chemAddQuestions([
["m12-a-001",12,25,1,"Concept","mcq","Which of the following pressure equalities is FALSE?",["1 atm = 760 mmHg","1 atm = 760 torr","1 atm = 14.7 kPa","1 atm = 101.325 kPa","1 atm = 1.01325 bar"],2,"1 atm = 760 mmHg = 760 torr = 101.325 kPa = 1.01325 bar are all true. 14.7 is the number of POUNDS PER SQUARE INCH (psi) in 1 atm, not kilopascals - 1 atm is 101.325 kPa. The trap is remembering the number 14.7 without its unit.",["Four of these are on the course constants sheet.","Torr and mmHg are the same size unit; a kilopascal is 1000 Pa.","14.7 belongs to a non-SI unit (psi)."]],
["m12-a-002",12,25,2,"Calculation","mcq","A mercury barometer reads 745 mmHg. What is the atmospheric pressure in atmospheres?",["0.980 atm","1.02 atm","5.66 × 10⁵ atm","7.35 atm","0.745 atm"],0,"P = 745 mmHg × (1 atm / 760 mmHg) = 0.980 atm. A pressure a little below 760 mmHg must come out a little below 1 atm. 1.02 atm is the factor upside down, 5.66 × 10⁵ multiplied instead of dividing, 7.35 used the kPa factor, and 0.745 just converted mm to m.",["Which unit conversion factor links mmHg and atm?","1 atm = 760 mmHg; arrange it so mmHg cancels.","745 ÷ 760."]],
["m12-a-003",12,25,2,"Calculation","num","Convert a pressure of 0.875 atm to kilopascals. (Enter a number in kPa.)",["88.7 kPa"],88.659375,"0.875 atm × (101.325 kPa / 1 atm) = 88.7 kPa (3 sig figs from 0.875).",["Look up how many kPa equal 1 atm.","1 atm = 101.325 kPa.","Multiply 0.875 by 101.325."]],
["m12-a-004",12,25,3,"Calculation","mcq","A weather report gives the air pressure as 98.6 kPa. What is this pressure in mmHg?",["1.30 × 10⁻¹ mmHg","1.31 × 10¹ mmHg","7.40 × 10² mmHg","7.81 × 10² mmHg","7.49 × 10⁴ mmHg"],2,"Go through atm: 98.6 kPa × (1 atm / 101.325 kPa) × (760 mmHg / 1 atm) = 740. mmHg. Sanity check: 98.6 kPa is just under 1 atm, so the answer must be just under 760 mmHg. The other choices skip the kPa → atm step (7.49 × 10⁴), divide where they should multiply, or invert a factor.",["Is 98.6 kPa more or less than 1 atm? So should the answer be more or less than 760?","Two factors: kPa → atm, then atm → mmHg.","98.6 ÷ 101.325 × 760."]],
["m12-a-005",12,25,1,"Concept","mcq","A mercury barometer is a long glass tube, closed at the top, filled with mercury and inverted into a dish of mercury. Which change would change the VERTICAL height of the mercury column?",["Using a tube with twice the diameter","Tilting the tube at an angle","Using a longer tube","Pouring more mercury into the dish","Carrying the barometer to the top of a mountain"],4,"The column is held up by atmospheric pressure pushing on the mercury in the dish, so its vertical height depends only on the outside pressure (and the density of the liquid). On a mountain the air pressure is lower, so the column falls. Pressure is force per area, so a wider tube has more weight but also more area - no change. Tilting lengthens the column along the glass but the vertical height is the same; a longer tube or more mercury in the dish changes nothing.",["What pushes the mercury up the tube?","The column height balances the outside air pressure.","Which choice changes the air pressure itself?"]],
["m12-a-006",12,25,2,"Calculation","mcq","Mercury (d = 13.6 g/mL) stands 760 mm high in a barometer at 1 atm. How tall would the column be at 1 atm if the barometer used water (d = 1.00 g/mL) instead?",["0.0559 m","0.760 m","1.03 m","10.3 m","103 m"],3,"The same pressure supports a column whose height is inversely proportional to the liquid's density: h(water) = 760 mm × (13.6 / 1.00) = 1.03 × 10⁴ mm = 10.3 m. Water is less dense, so the column must be TALLER - which is why barometers use mercury. Dividing by 13.6 gives a column shorter than mercury's, which is backwards.",["Will a less dense liquid need a taller or shorter column to balance the same pressure?","h × d is the same for both liquids.","760 mm × 13.6, then convert mm to m."]],
["m12-a-007",12,25,3,"Calculation","mcq","A flask of gas is connected to an open-end mercury manometer; the other arm is open to the air at 755 mmHg. The mercury in the OPEN arm stands 52 mm HIGHER than the mercury in the arm attached to the flask. What is the gas pressure in atm?",["0.925 atm","0.0684 atm","0.993 atm","1.06 atm","807 atm"],3,"The gas has pushed the mercury away from the flask and up the open arm, so the gas pressure is GREATER than the air's by 52 mmHg: P = 755 + 52 = 807 mmHg = 807/760 = 1.06 atm. Subtracting (0.925 atm) gets the direction backwards; 52 mmHg alone would be a closed-end reading; 807 atm forgot to convert.",["Which side is pushing harder - the gas or the atmosphere?","Open-end: P(gas) = P(atm) ± Δh; the gas side is the side the mercury is pushed away from.","755 + 52 mmHg, then divide by 760."]],
["m12-a-008",12,25,3,"Calculation","mcq","An open-end mercury manometer is attached to a flask. Atmospheric pressure is 763 mmHg, and the mercury in the arm attached to the FLASK is 38 mm higher than in the open arm. What is the gas pressure in kPa?",["96.7 kPa","107 kPa","5.07 kPa","102 kPa","725 kPa"],0,"The atmosphere has pushed the mercury toward the flask, so the gas is at LOWER pressure than the air: P = 763 − 38 = 725 mmHg × (101.325 kPa / 760 mmHg) = 96.7 kPa. Adding the 38 mm gets the direction backwards; 725 kPa forgot that mmHg is not kPa.",["The mercury is higher on the flask side - who pushed it there?","P(gas) = P(atm) − Δh when the flask side is higher.","725 mmHg × 101.325/760."]],
["m12-a-009",12,25,2,"Calculation","mcq","In a CLOSED-end manometer (vacuum above the mercury in the closed arm) attached to a flask, the mercury in the closed arm is 225 mm higher than in the flask arm. What is the gas pressure?",["1.30 atm","0.704 atm","3.38 atm","0.225 atm","0.296 atm"],4,"With a vacuum on the closed side, nothing pushes back except the mercury itself, so the height difference IS the gas pressure: P = 225 mmHg = 225/760 = 0.296 atm. Atmospheric pressure does not enter a closed-end reading - adding or subtracting 760 is the open-end habit applied to the wrong device.",["What is above the mercury in the closed arm?","For a closed-end manometer P(gas) = Δh.","225 ÷ 760."]],
["m12-a-010",12,25,2,"Calculation","mcq","A 2.50 L sample of gas at 1.20 atm is compressed to 0.800 L at constant temperature. What is the new pressure?",["0.384 atm","3.75 atm","1.20 atm","2.04 atm","3.12 atm"],1,"Boyle's law (n and T fixed): P₁V₁ = P₂V₂ → P₂ = (1.20 atm)(2.50 L)/(0.800 L) = 3.75 atm. Squeezing the gas into a smaller volume must RAISE the pressure; 0.384 atm inverts the volume ratio.",["Smaller volume at constant T: does P go up or down?","P₁V₁ = P₂V₂.","P₂ = 1.20 × 2.50 / 0.800."]],
["m12-a-011",12,25,1,"Concept","mcq","For a fixed amount of gas at constant temperature, which plot gives a straight line passing through the origin?",["P versus V","V versus P","P versus 1/V","1/P versus 1/V","PV versus V"],2,"Boyle: PV = constant, so P = k(1/V) - P plotted against 1/V is a straight line through the origin. P versus V (or V versus P) is a curve (hyperbola); 1/P versus 1/V is also a hyperbola; PV versus V is a horizontal line that does not pass through the origin.",["Write Boyle’s law as P = something.","PV = k means P = k × (1/V).","y = kx is a line through the origin - which variables are y and x?"]],
["m12-a-012",12,25,2,"Calculation","mcq","A balloon holds 455 mL of gas at 22 °C. It is heated to 98 °C at constant pressure. What is the new volume?",["2.03 × 10³ mL","572 mL","531 mL","362 mL","102 mL"],1,"Charles's law needs kelvin: T₁ = 22 + 273.15 = 295.15 K, T₂ = 98 + 273.15 = 371.15 K. V₂ = 455 mL × (371.15/295.15) = 572 mL. Using °C (2.03 × 10³ mL) multiplies the volume by more than 4 - the kelvin trap. 362 mL inverts the ratio (heating must expand the gas).",["Heating at constant pressure: bigger or smaller?","V₁/T₁ = V₂/T₂ with T in kelvin.","455 × 371.15 / 295.15."]],
["m12-a-013",12,25,4,"Trap","mcq","A sample of gas at 10 °C is heated to 20 °C at constant pressure. What happens to its volume?",["It doubles","It increases by exactly 10%","It halves","It increases by about 3.5%","It does not change, because pressure is constant"],3,"Volume is proportional to KELVIN temperature: V₂/V₁ = (20 + 273.15)/(10 + 273.15) = 293.15/283.15 = 1.0353, an increase of about 3.5%. \"Doubles\" treats 10 → 20 °C as doubling the temperature, but °C has an arbitrary zero. Constant pressure is exactly the condition for Charles's law, so the volume does change.",["Is 20 °C really twice as hot as 10 °C?","Convert both temperatures to kelvin.","Compute 293.15/283.15."]],
["m12-a-014",12,25,2,"Calculation","num","A rigid steel tank contains air at 2.20 atm and 15 °C. It sits in the sun until the air is at 45 °C. What is the new pressure in atm? (Enter a number in atm.)",["2.43 atm"],2.429047371160854,"Rigid tank: V and n fixed, so Gay-Lussac's law P₁/T₁ = P₂/T₂ in KELVIN. P₂ = 2.20 atm × (318.15 K / 288.15 K) = 2.43 atm. Using °C would give 6.60 atm - a tripling the real tank never sees.",["Which two variables change? Which stay fixed?","P/T is constant for fixed n and V; T in kelvin.","2.20 × 318.15 / 288.15."]],
["m12-a-015",12,25,4,"Trap","mcq","An aerosol can contains gas at 1.50 atm at 25 °C. It will burst if the internal pressure reaches 3.00 atm. Treating the can as rigid, at what temperature (°C) will it burst?",["−124 °C","50.0 °C","323 °C","596 °C","869 °C"],2,"Doubling the pressure of a rigid container doubles the KELVIN temperature: T₂ = 298.15 K × (3.00/1.50) = 596.3 K = 323 °C. Doubling the °C value (50.0 °C) is the classic trap; 596 °C forgot to convert back from kelvin, and 869 °C added 273 instead of subtracting.",["Is P proportional to °C or to K?","P₁/T₁ = P₂/T₂ with T in K, then convert the answer back to °C.","T₂ = 2 × 298.15 K; subtract 273.15."]],
["m12-a-016",12,25,2,"Calculation","mcq","A balloon holds 0.220 mol of helium in 5.00 L. Another 0.110 mol of helium is added at the same temperature and pressure. What is the new volume?",["2.50 L","7.50 L","10.0 L","3.33 L","5.00 L"],1,"Avogadro's law: V/n is constant at fixed T and P. n₂ = 0.220 + 0.110 = 0.330 mol, so V₂ = 5.00 L × (0.330/0.220) = 7.50 L. 10.0 L treats \"added 0.110\" as doubling; 3.33 L inverts the ratio; 2.50 L subtracts.",["More gas at the same T and P takes up more room.","V₁/n₁ = V₂/n₂ - use the TOTAL moles after the addition.","5.00 × 0.330/0.220."]],
["m12-a-017",12,25,3,"Calculation","mcq","A gas sample occupies 1.75 L at 25 °C and 745 torr. What volume would it occupy at STP?",["1.57 L","1.60 L","1.64 L","1.72 L","1.87 L"],0,"Combined gas law: V₂ = V₁ × (P₁/P₂) × (T₂/T₁) = 1.75 L × (745/760) × (273.15/298.15) = 1.57 L. Both corrections shrink the volume here (the pressure rises to 760 torr and the gas cools). Each wrong choice flips one ratio or leaves one correction out.",["STP = 0 °C and 760 torr. Will each change make the gas bigger or smaller?","P₁V₁/T₁ = P₂V₂/T₂, T in kelvin.","1.75 × (745/760) × (273.15/298.15)."]],
["m12-a-018",12,25,3,"Calculation","num","A weather balloon holds 12.0 L of helium at 1.00 atm and 22 °C. It rises to an altitude where the pressure is 0.310 atm and the temperature is −35 °C. What is its volume there in liters? (Enter a number in L.)",["31.2 L"],31.233981627712534,"V₂ = V₁ × (P₁/P₂) × (T₂/T₁) = 12.0 L × (1.00/0.310) × (238.15 K / 295.15 K) = 31.2 L. The pressure drop makes it expand a lot; the cold makes it shrink a little. −35 °C must become 238.15 K before it goes into a ratio.",["Two things change: P and T.","P₁V₁/T₁ = P₂V₂/T₂ with kelvin temperatures.","12.0 × (1.00/0.310) × (238.15/295.15)."]],
["m12-a-019",12,25,2,"Calculation","num","How many moles of gas are in a 3.25 L flask at 1.12 atm and 27 °C? (Enter a number in mol.)",["0.148 mol"],0.14778539725181689,"n = PV/RT = (1.12 atm)(3.25 L) / [(0.08206 L·atm/(mol·K))(300.15 K)] = 0.148 mol.",["Which equation links P, V, n and T?","n = PV/RT, R = 0.08206 L·atm/(mol·K), T in K.","(1.12 × 3.25)/(0.08206 × 300.15)."]],
["m12-a-020",12,25,2,"Calculation","mcq","What volume does 0.500 mol of N₂ occupy at 35 °C and 0.850 atm?",["1.69 L","10.7 L","11.2 L","14.9 L","1.51 × 10³ L"],3,"V = nRT/P = (0.500 mol)(0.08206)(308.15 K)/(0.850 atm) = 14.9 L. 1.69 L used 35 instead of 308.15 K; 1.51 × 10³ L used R = 8.314 with atm and L (units do not match); 10.7 L multiplied by P; 11.2 L is half of 22.4 - the STP molar volume, but this gas is not at STP.",["Not STP, so 22.4 L/mol does not apply.","V = nRT/P with the L·atm value of R.","0.500 × 0.08206 × 308.15 / 0.850."]],
["m12-a-021",12,25,3,"Calculation","mcq","What is the pressure exerted by 4.40 g of CO₂ in a 2.00 L container at 25 °C?",["0.103 atm","1.22 atm","2.45 atm","53.8 atm","124 atm"],1,"n = 4.40 g ÷ 44.01 g/mol = 0.09998 mol. P = nRT/V = (0.09998)(0.08206)(298.15)/(2.00) = 1.22 atm. The traps: grams used as moles (53.8 atm), 25 °C left unconverted, R = 8.314 with L and atm, or forgetting to divide by V.",["PV = nRT needs moles, not grams.","Convert 4.40 g CO₂ to mol first (M = 44.01 g/mol).","P = 0.1000 × 0.08206 × 298.15 / 2.00."]],
["m12-a-022",12,25,3,"Calculation","num","A 0.250 mol sample of gas fills a 5.00 L container at 1.50 atm. What is its temperature in degrees Celsius? (Enter a number in °C.)",["92.4 °C"],92.436156470875,"T = PV/nR = (1.50)(5.00)/[(0.250)(0.08206)] = 365.6 K. The question asks for °C: 365.6 − 273.15 = 92.4 °C. Entering the kelvin value is the trap.",["Solve PV = nRT for T.","T comes out in kelvin; the question wants °C.","T = 7.50/(0.250 × 0.08206), then subtract 273.15."]],
["m12-a-023",12,25,3,"Calculation","mcq","A 12.0 L steel cylinder holds oxygen gas at 155 atm and 21 °C. What mass of O₂ is in the cylinder?",["24.3 g","77.1 g","1.23 × 10³ g","3.45 × 10⁴ g","2.47 × 10³ g"],4,"n = PV/RT = (155)(12.0)/[(0.08206)(294.15)] = 77.06 mol O₂; mass = 77.06 mol × 32.00 g/mol = 2.47 × 10³ g. Using 16.00 g/mol forgets that oxygen gas is diatomic O₂; 77.1 g stops at moles; the others used 21 K or the J-unit R.",["First find moles with PV = nRT.","T = 294.15 K; then multiply by the molar mass of O₂ (not O).","n ≈ 77.1 mol; × 32.00 g/mol."]],
["m12-a-024",12,25,1,"Concept","mcq","Standard temperature and pressure (STP) for gases in this course means:",["25 °C and 1 atm","0 °C and 1 atm","0 K and 1 atm","0 °C and 0 atm","100 °C and 1 atm"],1,"STP is 0 °C (273.15 K) and 1 atm, where 1 mol of ideal gas occupies 22.4 L. 25 °C and 1 atm are the conditions for STANDARD STATES in thermochemistry (ΔH°f) - a different convention, and the most common mix-up.",["It is the condition where molar volume is 22.4 L.","The temperature is the freezing point of water.","273.15 K and 760 torr."]],
["m12-a-025",12,25,2,"Calculation","mcq","How many moles of helium are in 5.60 L of He at STP?",["4.00 mol","125 mol","0.0625 mol","1.40 mol","0.250 mol"],4,"At STP 1 mol occupies 22.4 L: n = 5.60 L ÷ 22.4 L/mol = 0.250 mol. 4.00 mol is 22.4/5.60 (upside down); 125 mol multiplies; dividing by 4.003 g/mol mixes up volume and mass.",["What volume does one mole of gas occupy at STP?","n = V ÷ 22.4 L/mol.","5.60 ÷ 22.4."]],
["m12-a-026",12,25,4,"Trap","mcq","How many moles of gas are in 5.60 L at 25 °C and 1.00 atm?",["0.229 mol","0.250 mol","0.273 mol","2.73 mol","4.00 mol"],0,"This is NOT STP (25 °C, not 0 °C), so 22.4 L/mol does not apply. n = PV/RT = (1.00)(5.60)/[(0.08206)(298.15)] = 0.229 mol. 0.250 mol is the STP shortcut applied at the wrong temperature; 0.273 mol corrected the temperature in the wrong direction (a warmer gas has FEWER moles in the same volume).",["Check the temperature before reaching for 22.4 L/mol.","Use n = PV/RT at 298.15 K.","5.60/(0.08206 × 298.15)."]],
["m12-a-027",12,25,2,"Calculation","mcq","What is the density of CO₂ gas at STP?",["0.0194 g/L","0.509 g/L","1.43 g/L","1.80 g/L","1.96 g/L"],4,"d = PM/RT = (1.00 atm)(44.01 g/mol)/[(0.08206)(273.15 K)] = 1.96 g/L (the same as 44.01 g ÷ 22.4 L). 1.80 g/L used 25 °C; 0.509 g/L is the formula upside down (that is L per gram); 0.0194 used the J-unit R; 1.43 g/L used the molar mass of O₂.",["Density of a gas: d = PM/RT.","At STP you can also divide the molar mass by 22.4 L.","44.01 ÷ 22.4."]],
["m12-a-028",12,25,3,"Calculation","num","Sulfur hexafluoride, SF₆, is a very dense gas. What is its density at 0.950 atm and 35 °C? (Enter a number in g/L.)",["5.49 g/L"],5.487331744651329,"M(SF₆) = 32.06 + 6(19.00) = 146.06 g/mol. d = PM/RT = (0.950)(146.06)/[(0.08206)(308.15)] = 5.49 g/L - about five times denser than air.",["d = PM/RT.","Find the molar mass of SF₆ and convert 35 °C to K.","0.950 × 146.06 / (0.08206 × 308.15)."]],
["m12-a-029",12,25,3,"Calculation","mcq","An unknown gas has a density of 2.39 g/L at 25 °C and 0.910 atm. Which gas is it?",["O₂","Cl₂","SO₃","CO₂","SO₂"],4,"M = dRT/P = (2.39 g/L)(0.08206)(298.15 K)/(0.910 atm) = 64.3 g/mol, which matches SO₂ (64.06 g/mol). O₂ is 32.00, CO₂ 44.01, Cl₂ 70.90 and SO₃ 80.06 g/mol.",["Rearrange d = PM/RT for M.","M = dRT/P with T in kelvin.","You should get about 64 g/mol."]],
["m12-a-030",12,25,3,"Calculation","num","A volatile liquid is vaporized in a 354 mL flask at 100.0 °C and 750. torr; the vapor that fills the flask has a mass of 0.846 g. What is the molar mass of the compound? (Enter a number in g/mol.)",["74.2 g/mol"],74.15396685288135,"Convert first: P = 750./760 = 0.9868 atm, V = 0.354 L, T = 373.15 K. n = PV/RT = (0.9868)(0.354)/[(0.08206)(373.15)] = 1.141 × 10⁻² mol. M = 0.846 g ÷ 1.141 × 10⁻² mol = 74.2 g/mol (diethyl ether is 74.12).",["Molar mass = grams ÷ moles; get moles from PV = nRT.","Convert torr → atm, mL → L, °C → K before using R = 0.08206.","n ≈ 0.01141 mol."]],
["m12-a-031",12,25,1,"Concept","mcq","At the same temperature and pressure, which gas has the greatest density?",["He","CH₄","SF₆","N₂","CO₂"],2,"At fixed T and P, d = PM/RT is proportional to molar mass M. SF₆ (146 g/mol) beats CO₂ (44), N₂ (28), CH₄ (16) and He (4).",["Look at d = PM/RT - what differs between the gases?","Density is proportional to molar mass at the same T and P.","Find the heaviest molecule."]],
["m12-a-032",12,25,4,"Exam","mcq","A hydrocarbon is 85.7% C and 14.3% H by mass, and its vapor has a density of 2.50 g/L at STP. What is its molecular formula?",["C₄H₈","CH₂","C₂H₄","C₃H₆","C₅H₁₀"],0,"Empirical formula: 85.7 g C ÷ 12.01 = 7.14 mol; 14.3 g H ÷ 1.008 = 14.2 mol; ratio 1 : 2, so CH₂ (14.03 g/mol). Molar mass from the density: M = dRT/P = 2.50 × 0.08206 × 273.15 / 1.00 = 56.0 g/mol (or 2.50 × 22.4). 56.0 ÷ 14.03 = 4.00 ≈ 4, so the formula is C₄H₈. CH₂ is only the empirical formula.",["Two pieces: an empirical formula from the percentages, and a molar mass from the density.","At STP, M = d × 22.4 L/mol.","Divide that molar mass by the empirical-formula mass of CH₂."]],
["m12-a-033",12,25,2,"Concept","mcq","A hot-air balloon rises because heating the air inside it at constant pressure:",["increases the number of moles of air inside the balloon","raises the molar mass of the air","raises the pressure inside far above the outside pressure","lowers the air’s density, since d = PM/RT falls as T rises","makes the air molecules lighter"],3,"d = PM/RT: at the same P and M, a higher T means a lower density. The balloon is open at the bottom, so the hot air inside is less dense than the cooler air outside and the balloon floats. The molecules are not lighter and M does not change; heating actually pushes some air OUT, lowering n.",["Which variable in d = PM/RT is the burner changing?","Density is inversely proportional to T at fixed P.","Less dense air floats on denser air."]],
["m12-a-034",12,25,4,"Trap","mcq","A sealed rigid container holds gas at 1.00 atm and 27 °C. It is heated to 327 °C. What is the new pressure?",["0.0826 atm","0.500 atm","1.00 atm","2.00 atm","12.1 atm"],3,"In kelvin: 27 °C = 300.15 K and 327 °C = 600.15 K - the absolute temperature DOUBLES. P₂ = 1.00 atm × 600.15/300.15 = 2.00 atm. 12.1 atm uses the °C ratio, the trap the numbers were chosen to bait.",["Convert both temperatures to kelvin before comparing them.","P/T is constant in a rigid, sealed container.","What is 600.15/300.15?"]],
["m12-a-035",12,25,2,"Concept","mcq","For a fixed amount of gas, the pressure AND the kelvin temperature are both doubled. What happens to the volume?",["It doubles","It quadruples","It halves","It falls to one-quarter","It stays the same"],4,"V = nRT/P. Doubling T doubles V; doubling P halves it. The two cancel: V is unchanged.",["Write V in terms of n, T and P.","V ∝ T/P for fixed n.","(2T)/(2P) = T/P."]],
["m12-a-036",12,25,3,"Concept","mcq","The moles of a gas sample are doubled, its kelvin temperature is halved, and its pressure is doubled. The new volume is what fraction of the original?",["Unchanged","One-half","Double","Four times","One-quarter"],1,"V = nRT/P, so V₂/V₁ = (2)(½)/(2) = ½. The doubled moles and halved temperature cancel, leaving only the doubled pressure, which halves V.",["V ∝ nT/P.","Multiply the factors for n and T, divide by the factor for P.","(2 × ½) ÷ 2."]],
["m12-a-037",12,25,4,"Exam","mcq","Which sample contains the greatest number of gas molecules? (Every sample has a volume of 1.0 L.)",["H₂ at STP","CO₂ at STP","N₂ at 273 °C and 1.0 atm","O₂ at 0 °C and 2.0 atm","He at 25 °C and 1.0 atm"],3,"n = PV/RT, and the identity of the gas does not matter. At the same V, n ∝ P/T: O₂ at 2.0 atm and 273 K has P/T twice that of STP, so it has the most molecules. H₂ and CO₂ at STP tie (Avogadro), N₂ at 546 K has half as many as STP, and He at 298 K has slightly fewer than STP. The heavy CO₂ molecule is a distractor - molar mass does not affect the count.",["Does the kind of gas change how many molecules fit in 1 L?","n = PV/RT, so compare P/T for each sample.","Which sample has the highest pressure and the lowest temperature?"]],
["m12-a-038",12,25,2,"Calculation","mcq","What is the molar volume of an ideal gas at 25 °C and 1.00 atm?",["2.48 × 10³ L/mol","24.5 L/mol","22.4 L/mol","2.05 L/mol","0.0409 L/mol"],1,"V/n = RT/P = (0.08206)(298.15)/1.00 = 24.5 L/mol. 22.4 L/mol belongs to 0 °C (STP); a warmer gas takes up more room. 2.05 used 25 K, and 2.48 × 10³ used R = 8.314.",["Molar volume = V/n.","From PV = nRT, V/n = RT/P.","0.08206 × 298.15."]],
["m12-a-039",12,25,2,"Calculation","num","What volume does 8.00 g of O₂ occupy at STP? (Enter a number in L.)",["5.60 L"],5.6,"n = 8.00 g ÷ 32.00 g/mol = 0.250 mol; at STP V = 0.250 mol × 22.4 L/mol = 5.60 L. Using 16.00 g/mol (O instead of O₂) doubles the answer.",["Grams → moles first.","Molar mass of O₂ is 32.00 g/mol; 22.4 L/mol at STP.","0.250 mol × 22.4 L/mol."]],
["m12-a-040",12,25,2,"Calculation","mcq","Which gas has a density of 0.714 g/L at STP?",["NH₃","He","CH₄","Ne","N₂"],2,"M = d × 22.4 L/mol = 0.714 × 22.4 = 16.0 g/mol, which is CH₄ (16.04). NH₃ is 17.03 g/mol (density 0.760 g/L), Ne 20.18, N₂ 28.02, He 4.003.",["Density × molar volume = molar mass.","At STP the molar volume is 22.4 L/mol.","Which gas has M ≈ 16.0 g/mol?"]],
["m12-a-041",12,25,2,"Calculation","num","The air pressure on a high plateau is 640. torr. Express it in kilopascals. (Enter a number in kPa.)",["85.3 kPa"],85.32631578947368,"640. torr × (1 atm/760 torr) × (101.325 kPa/1 atm) = 85.3 kPa.",["Go through atmospheres.","760 torr = 1 atm = 101.325 kPa.","640 × 101.325 / 760."]],
["m12-a-042",12,26,4,"Exam","mcq","Automobile airbags inflate by the reaction 2NaN₃(s) → 2Na(s) + 3N₂(g). What mass of NaN₃ is needed to fill a 65.0 L airbag with N₂ at 1.15 atm and 25 °C?",["132 g","126 g","199 g","298 g","1.58 × 10³ g"],0,"n(N₂) = PV/RT = (1.15)(65.0)/[(0.08206)(298.15)] = 3.055 mol. Mole ratio: 3.055 mol N₂ × (2 mol NaN₃/3 mol N₂) = 2.037 mol NaN₃. M(NaN₃) = 22.99 + 3(14.01) = 65.02 g/mol, so mass = 132 g. Skipping the 2/3 ratio, inverting it (3/2), using 25 K, or using 22.4 L/mol (not STP) gives the other choices.",["Gas volume → moles of N₂ → moles of NaN₃ → grams.","PV = nRT for the N₂, then the 2 : 3 coefficient ratio.","n(N₂) ≈ 3.06 mol; × 2/3 × 65.02 g/mol."]],
["m12-a-043",12,26,3,"Calculation","num","Zinc reacts with excess hydrochloric acid: Zn(s) + 2HCl(aq) → ZnCl₂(aq) + H₂(g). What volume of H₂, measured at 22 °C and 0.985 atm, forms from 3.27 g of Zn? (Enter a number in L.)",["1.23 L"],1.229818172402495,"n(Zn) = 3.27/65.38 = 0.05002 mol = n(H₂) (1 : 1). V = nRT/P = (0.05002)(0.08206)(295.15)/0.985 = 1.23 L. HCl's coefficient of 2 does not matter - zinc is limiting and H₂ is 1 : 1 with Zn.",["Grams of Zn → mol Zn → mol H₂.","Mole ratio Zn : H₂ is 1 : 1; then V = nRT/P.","n ≈ 0.0500 mol; T = 295.15 K."]],
["m12-a-044",12,26,2,"Calculation","mcq","For N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) → 2NH₃(g), what volume of NH₃ forms from 6.0 L of H₂ with excess N₂, all volumes measured at the same temperature and pressure?",["2.0 L","4.0 L","12 L","18 L","6.0 L"],1,"At the same T and P, volume is proportional to moles (Avogadro), so gas volumes react in the coefficient ratio. 6.0 L H₂ × (2 L NH₃/3 L H₂) = 4.0 L NH₃. No molar volume or PV = nRT is needed.",["At the same T and P, what are volumes proportional to?","Use the coefficients directly as a volume ratio.","6.0 × 2/3."]],
["m12-a-045",12,26,4,"Exam","mcq","In 2H₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2H₂O(g), 5.0 L of H₂ is mixed with 3.0 L of O₂ and they react completely. What is the total volume of gas afterward, at the same temperature and pressure (water stays a gas)?",["5.0 L","8.0 L","0.5 L","6.0 L","5.5 L"],4,"Volumes react in the coefficient ratio. 5.0 L H₂ needs only 2.5 L O₂, so H₂ is limiting and 0.5 L O₂ is left over. Product: 5.0 L H₂ × (2/2) = 5.0 L H₂O(g). Total = 5.0 + 0.5 = 5.5 L. 5.0 L forgets the leftover O₂; 6.0 L treats O₂ as limiting; 8.0 L ignores the reaction.",["Find the limiting reactant using the volume ratio 2 : 1.","Leftover gas still counts toward the total volume.","H₂ is limiting; add the water vapor formed to the O₂ left over."]],
["m12-a-046",12,26,3,"Calculation","mcq","Heating 15.0 g of CaCO₃ decomposes it completely: CaCO₃(s) → CaO(s) + CO₂(g). What volume of CO₂ is produced at STP?",["7.63 L","6.71 L","3.67 L","3.36 L","1.68 L"],3,"n(CaCO₃) = 15.0 g ÷ 100.09 g/mol = 0.1499 mol = n(CO₂) (1 : 1). At STP: V = 0.1499 × 22.4 = 3.36 L. 7.63 L treats the 15.0 g as CO₂; 3.67 L uses 25 °C instead of STP.",["Moles of CaCO₃ first.","1 mol CaCO₃ gives 1 mol CO₂; 22.4 L/mol at STP.","0.1499 mol × 22.4 L/mol."]],
["m12-a-047",12,26,2,"Calculation","mcq","Propane burns: C₃H₈(g) + 5O₂(g) → 3CO₂(g) + 4H₂O(g). What volume of O₂ is needed to burn 2.50 L of propane, both measured at the same temperature and pressure?",["2.50 L","0.500 L","7.50 L","12.5 L","10.0 L"],3,"Same T and P → volumes in the coefficient ratio: 2.50 L C₃H₈ × (5 L O₂/1 L C₃H₈) = 12.5 L O₂. 7.50 L and 10.0 L are the CO₂ and H₂O volumes; 0.500 L divides by 5.",["Volumes behave like moles here.","Use the C₃H₈ : O₂ coefficient ratio, 1 : 5.","2.50 × 5."]],
["m12-a-048",12,26,4,"Exam","mcq","What volume of CO₂, measured at 25 °C and 1.00 atm, is produced by burning 10.0 g of propane? C₃H₈(g) + 5O₂(g) → 3CO₂(g) + 4H₂O(l)",["16.6 L","1.40 L","5.55 L","15.2 L","22.2 L"],0,"n(C₃H₈) = 10.0 ÷ 44.09 = 0.2268 mol; × 3 mol CO₂/mol C₃H₈ = 0.6804 mol CO₂. V = nRT/P = (0.6804)(0.08206)(298.15)/1.00 = 16.6 L. Forgetting the 3, using 22.4 L/mol at 25 °C, using the water coefficient, or using 25 K gives the distractors.",["grams propane → mol propane → mol CO₂ → volume.","Coefficient ratio 3 CO₂ : 1 C₃H₈; then V = nRT/P at 298.15 K.","n(CO₂) ≈ 0.680 mol."]],
["m12-a-049",12,26,4,"Exam","mcq","For 4NH₃(g) + 5O₂(g) → 4NO(g) + 6H₂O(g), 10.0 L of NH₃ and 10.0 L of O₂ are mixed at the same temperature and pressure. What volume of NO can form?",["10.0 L","12.5 L","12.0 L","4.00 L","8.00 L"],4,"10.0 L NH₃ would need 10.0 × 5/4 = 12.5 L O₂, but only 10.0 L is present, so O₂ is limiting. NO formed = 10.0 L O₂ × (4 NO/5 O₂) = 8.00 L. 10.0 L assumes NH₃ is limiting; 12.0 L is the H₂O volume from 10.0 L O₂ (6/5).",["Equal volumes does not mean neither is limiting - compare with the coefficients.","Find which gas runs out first using the 4 : 5 ratio.","O₂ is limiting; use 4 NO per 5 O₂."]],
["m12-a-050",12,26,4,"Exam","mcq","A rigid flask at constant temperature contains CO at a partial pressure of 1.00 atm and O₂ at 0.80 atm. They react completely by 2CO(g) + O₂(g) → 2CO₂(g). What is the total pressure afterward?",["1.80 atm","1.00 atm","1.30 atm","0.30 atm","2.30 atm"],2,"In a rigid flask at constant T, partial pressure ∝ moles, so pressures react in the coefficient ratio. CO is limiting: 1.00 atm CO uses 0.50 atm O₂ and makes 1.00 atm CO₂. Left: 0.80 − 0.50 = 0.30 atm O₂. Total = 1.00 + 0.30 = 1.30 atm. 1.80 atm ignores the reaction (3 mol of gas become 2); 1.00 atm forgets the leftover O₂.",["At constant V and T, partial pressures act like moles.","Find the limiting reactant, then the products and leftovers.","P(CO₂) = 1.00 atm, P(O₂ left) = 0.30 atm."]],
["m12-a-051",12,26,3,"Calculation","mcq","A gas mixture contains 2.00 mol N₂ and 0.500 mol O₂ at a total pressure of 1.50 atm. What is the partial pressure of O₂?",["0.375 atm","1.20 atm","0.300 atm","0.750 atm","0.200 atm"],2,"X(O₂) = 0.500/(2.00 + 0.500) = 0.200. P(O₂) = X·P(total) = 0.200 × 1.50 = 0.300 atm. 0.375 atm used 0.500/2.00 (moles of N₂, not total) as the fraction; 1.20 atm is N₂'s partial pressure; 0.200 is the mole fraction itself.",["Partial pressure = mole fraction × total pressure.","The mole fraction divides by the TOTAL moles.","X(O₂) = 0.500/2.50."]],
["m12-a-052",12,26,3,"Calculation","num","A 10.0 L flask at 27 °C contains 4.00 g of He and 16.0 g of O₂. What is the partial pressure of helium? (Enter a number in atm.)",["2.46 atm"],2.461185011241568,"Each gas behaves as if it were alone: n(He) = 4.00/4.003 = 0.9993 mol, P(He) = nRT/V = (0.9993)(0.08206)(300.15)/10.0 = 2.46 atm. The O₂ (0.500 mol) only matters for the total pressure.",["Dalton: each gas fills the whole flask on its own.","P(He) = n(He)RT/V.","n(He) ≈ 0.999 mol; T = 300.15 K."]],
["m12-a-053",12,26,4,"Trap","mcq","Equal MASSES of H₂ and O₂ are placed in the same container. What is the ratio P(H₂)/P(O₂)?",["15.9","7.94","3.98","1.00","0.0630"],0,"Partial pressure is proportional to MOLES, not mass. For equal masses m: n(H₂)/n(O₂) = (m/2.016)/(m/32.00) = 32.00/2.016 = 15.9. 1.00 assumes equal masses give equal pressures; 3.98 borrows the square root from Graham's law.",["What is partial pressure proportional to?","Convert equal masses to moles - the lighter gas has more moles.","The ratio is M(O₂)/M(H₂)."]],
["m12-a-054",12,26,2,"Calculation","mcq","Air is 20.9 mol% O₂. On a mountain where the atmospheric pressure is 0.680 atm, what is the partial pressure of O₂?",["0.142 atm","0.209 atm","0.538 atm","0.680 atm","3.25 atm"],0,"P(O₂) = X(O₂)·P(total) = 0.209 × 0.680 atm = 0.142 atm. 0.209 atm is the sea-level value; 0.538 atm is the rest of the air (N₂ etc.). The PERCENT of O₂ is the same on the mountain - there is just less air.",["mol% ÷ 100 is the mole fraction.","Dalton: P(i) = X(i) × P(total).","0.209 × 0.680."]],
["m12-a-055",12,26,2,"Calculation","mcq","A mixture of N₂ and H₂ has a total pressure of 2.50 atm, and the partial pressure of N₂ is 0.65 atm. What is the mole fraction of H₂?",["0.260","1.85","0.35","3.8","0.740"],4,"P(H₂) = 2.50 − 0.65 = 1.85 atm. X(H₂) = P(H₂)/P(total) = 1.85/2.50 = 0.740 (1.85 and 2.50 both have three sig figs). 0.260 is N₂'s mole fraction; 1.85 is a pressure, not a fraction (a mole fraction can never exceed 1); 0.35 treats 0.65 atm as if it were a mole fraction; 3.8 is 2.50/0.65 upside down.",["First find H₂’s partial pressure.","X(i) = P(i)/P(total).","1.85 ÷ 2.50."]],
["m12-a-056",12,26,4,"Exam","mcq","A 2.00 L flask of He at 1.50 atm is joined by a valve to a 3.00 L flask of Ne at 0.800 atm. The valve is opened and the gases mix at constant temperature. What is the total pressure?",["0.480 atm","0.600 atm","1.08 atm","1.15 atm","2.30 atm"],2,"Each gas expands into the full 5.00 L (Boyle): P(He) = 1.50 × 2.00/5.00 = 0.600 atm; P(Ne) = 0.800 × 3.00/5.00 = 0.480 atm. Total = 1.08 atm. Adding 1.50 + 0.800 forgets the expansion; averaging them (1.15) ignores that the flasks have different sizes.",["What volume does each gas occupy after the valve opens?","Apply Boyle’s law to each gas separately, then add (Dalton).","P(He) = 0.600 atm."]],
["m12-a-057",12,26,4,"Trap","mcq","A gas mixture is 50.0% helium and 50.0% argon BY MASS, at a total pressure of 1.00 atm. What is the partial pressure of helium?",["0.909 atm","0.900 atm","0.500 atm","0.100 atm","0.0911 atm"],0,"Per 100.0 g: n(He) = 50.0/4.003 = 12.49 mol, n(Ar) = 50.0/39.95 = 1.252 mol. X(He) = 12.49/13.74 = 0.909, so P(He) = 0.909 atm. 0.500 atm treats mass % as mole %; 0.0911 atm is argon's share; 0.100 and 0.900 atm play with the ratio of the molar masses instead of computing moles.",["Partial pressures follow MOLE fractions, not mass fractions.","Assume 100 g: 50.0 g of each, convert to moles.","Helium has about ten times as many moles as argon."]],
["m12-a-058",12,26,3,"Calculation","mcq","A 0.250 L sample of O₂ is collected over water at 25 °C; the total pressure is 755 torr and the vapor pressure of water at 25 °C is 23.8 torr. How many moles of O₂ were collected?",["1.02 × 10⁻² mol","9.83 × 10⁻³ mol","1.05 × 10⁻² mol","0.117 mol","7.47 mol"],1,"The collected gas is wet: P(O₂) = 755 − 23.8 = 731.2 torr = 0.9621 atm. n = PV/RT = (0.9621)(0.250)/[(0.08206)(298.15)] = 9.83 × 10⁻³ mol. Forgetting to subtract the water vapor, adding it, leaving the pressure in torr, or using 25 K gives the other choices.",["The gas in the bottle is a mixture of O₂ and water vapor.","P(O₂) = P(total) − P(H₂O), then convert to atm.","P(O₂) = 731.2 torr."]],
["m12-a-059",12,26,4,"Exam","num","Heating KClO₃ releases oxygen: 2KClO₃(s) → 2KCl(s) + 3O₂(g). The O₂ is collected over water: 128 mL at 24 °C and a total pressure of 762 torr (vapor pressure of water at 24 °C = 22.4 torr). What mass of KClO₃ decomposed? (Enter a number in g.)",["0.417 g"],0.41735737208411267,"P(O₂) = 762 − 22.4 = 739.6 torr = 0.9732 atm. n(O₂) = PV/RT = (0.9732)(0.128 L)/[(0.08206)(297.15 K)] = 5.108 × 10⁻³ mol. n(KClO₃) = 5.108 × 10⁻³ × 2/3 = 3.406 × 10⁻³ mol. M(KClO₃) = 39.10 + 35.45 + 3(16.00) = 122.55 g/mol → 0.417 g.",["Dry-gas pressure first: subtract the water vapor.","PV = nRT gives mol O₂; then the 2 : 3 ratio back to KClO₃.","n(O₂) ≈ 5.11 × 10⁻³ mol."],{"tol":0.02}],
["m12-a-060",12,26,1,"Concept","mcq","Which statement is NOT a postulate of the kinetic-molecular theory of an ideal gas?",["The volume of the particles themselves is negligible compared with the container volume","Particles move randomly in straight lines between collisions","Gas particles attract one another strongly at all distances","Collisions are elastic: no net kinetic energy is lost","The average kinetic energy of the particles is proportional to the kelvin temperature"],2,"KMT assumes NO attractions or repulsions between particles (except during collisions). Real gases do attract one another, which is exactly why they deviate from ideal behavior at low temperature. The other four are the postulates.",["An ideal gas is the simplest possible model.","Which force would make the gas condense?","Ideal gas particles ignore each other except when they collide."]],
["m12-a-061",12,26,2,"Trap","mcq","A sample of He and a sample of Xe are both at 300 K. Which statement about the average kinetic energy of their atoms is correct?",["He atoms have a larger average kinetic energy because they move faster","Xe atoms have a larger average kinetic energy because they are heavier","They are equal","It depends on the pressure of each sample","It depends on the volume of each container"],2,"Average kinetic energy depends ONLY on the kelvin temperature: KE(avg) = (3/2)(R/N_A)T. At the same T, every gas has the same average KE. He atoms are faster but lighter; Xe atoms are slower but heavier - ½mv² comes out the same.",["What single variable does average KE depend on?","KMT: average KE ∝ T (in K).","Same T means the same average KE."]],
["m12-a-062",12,26,2,"Calculation","mcq","The temperature of a gas is raised from 300 K to 1200 K. By what factor does its rms speed change?",["× 2","× 4","× 16","× √2","× ½"],0,"u(rms) = √(3RT/M) ∝ √T. T increases by 1200/300 = 4, so u(rms) increases by √4 = 2. × 4 is the change in average KE, not speed.",["How does rms speed depend on T?","u(rms) ∝ √T.","√(1200/300)."]],
["m12-a-063",12,26,4,"Trap","mcq","A gas is heated from 100 °C to 200 °C. By what factor does the average kinetic energy of its molecules increase?",["0.789","1.27","1.13","2.00","4.00"],1,"Average KE ∝ KELVIN temperature: 473.15/373.15 = 1.27. \"Doubles\" uses °C, which has an arbitrary zero. 1.13 is the factor for the rms SPEED, not the kinetic energy.",["Is 200 °C twice as hot as 100 °C?","KE(avg) ∝ T in kelvin.","473.15/373.15."]],
["m12-a-064",12,26,3,"Calculation","num","Calculate the root-mean-square speed of N₂ molecules at 25 °C. (Enter a number in m/s.)",["515 m/s"],515.168118751693,"u(rms) = √(3RT/M) with R = 8.314 J/(mol·K) and M in kg/mol: M(N₂) = 28.02 g/mol = 0.02802 kg/mol. u = √[3(8.314)(298.15)/0.02802] = 515 m/s. (A J is kg·m²/s², which is why M must be in kg.)",["Which R and which mass unit does the rms formula need?","R = 8.314 J/(mol·K); M = 0.02802 kg/mol; T = 298.15 K.","√(3 × 8.314 × 298.15 / 0.02802)."]],
["m12-a-065",12,26,3,"Calculation","mcq","What is the rms speed of helium atoms at 27 °C?",["43.2 m/s","410. m/s","790. m/s","1.37 × 10³ m/s","1.87 × 10⁶ m/s"],3,"u(rms) = √(3RT/M) = √[3(8.314)(300.15)/0.004003] = 1.37 × 10³ m/s. The traps: M in g/mol (43.2 m/s), 27 K instead of 300.15 K, forgetting the square root, or dropping the 3.",["M must be in kg/mol.","He: 4.003 g/mol = 0.004003 kg/mol; T = 300.15 K.","√(3 × 8.314 × 300.15 / 0.004003)."]],
["m12-a-066",12,26,2,"Concept","mcq","Rank these gases in order of INCREASING rms speed at the same temperature: He, N₂, Ar, SO₂.",["He < N₂ < Ar < SO₂","SO₂ < Ar < N₂ < He","He < Ar < N₂ < SO₂","SO₂ < N₂ < Ar < He","N₂ < He < SO₂ < Ar"],1,"At the same T, u(rms) ∝ 1/√M: the heaviest molecule is slowest. M: SO₂ 64.06, Ar 39.95, N₂ 28.02, He 4.003. So SO₂ < Ar < N₂ < He. The reverse order is the \"heavier is faster\" mistake.",["Same T means the same average KE - so which gas must move fastest?","u(rms) ∝ 1/√M.","Order the molar masses from largest to smallest."]],
["m12-a-067",12,26,3,"Calculation","mcq","At the same temperature, what is the ratio of the rms speed of H₂ to that of O₂?",["0.0630","0.251","1.00","3.98","15.9"],3,"u ∝ 1/√M, so u(H₂)/u(O₂) = √(M(O₂)/M(H₂)) = √(32.00/2.016) = 3.98. 15.9 forgets the square root; 0.251 inverts the ratio (H₂ must be faster); 1.00 confuses equal KE with equal speed.",["Which molecule is faster?","u(rms) = √(3RT/M) - the ratio keeps only the M’s.","√(32.00/2.016)."]],
["m12-a-068",12,26,2,"Calculation","mcq","How many times faster does helium effuse than methane, CH₄, under the same conditions?",["0.250","0.500","1.00","2.00","4.01"],3,"Graham's law: rate(He)/rate(CH₄) = √(M(CH₄)/M(He)) = √(16.04/4.003) = 2.00. Without the square root you get 4.01; the inverted ratios would make the heavier gas faster.",["Lighter gases effuse faster.","rate₁/rate₂ = √(M₂/M₁).","√(16.04/4.003)."]],
["m12-a-069",12,26,3,"Calculation","mcq","An unknown gas effuses at 0.355 times the rate of helium under the same conditions. Which gas could it be?",["N₂","CH₄","Ar","CO₂","O₂"],4,"rate(X)/rate(He) = √(M(He)/M(X)) = 0.355 → M(X) = 4.003/(0.355)² = 31.8 g/mol ≈ O₂ (32.00). N₂ is 28.02, CH₄ 16.04, Ar 39.95, CO₂ 44.01. Forgetting to square 0.355 gives 11.3 g/mol, which matches nothing.",["Slower than He means heavier than He.","Square both sides of Graham’s law.","M = 4.003 ÷ 0.355²."]],
["m12-a-070",12,26,3,"Calculation","num","A sample of N₂ takes 38.0 s to effuse through a pinhole. An equal amount of an unknown gas takes 82.6 s under the same conditions. What is the molar mass of the unknown gas? (Enter a number in g/mol.)",["132 g/mol"],132.39178337950136,"Effusion TIME is inversely proportional to rate, so time ∝ √M: t(X)/t(N₂) = √(M(X)/M(N₂)). M(X) = 28.02 × (82.6/38.0)² = 132 g/mol (close to xenon, 131.29). Treating time like rate would give a molar mass smaller than N₂'s - impossible for a slower gas.",["A gas that takes longer is heavier.","Time ∝ √M (the inverse of rate).","M = 28.02 × (82.6/38.0)²."]],
["m12-a-071",12,26,4,"Exam","mcq","Uranium is enriched by effusion of UF₆. Given molar masses ²³⁵UF₆ = 349.04 g/mol and ²³⁸UF₆ = 352.05 g/mol, what is the ratio rate(²³⁵UF₆)/rate(²³⁸UF₆) for one effusion step?",["0.9957","1.0043","1.0064","1.0086","1.0128"],1,"rate(235)/rate(238) = √(352.05/349.04) = 1.0043. The separation per step is tiny, which is why enrichment plants need thousands of stages. 1.0086 skips the square root; 1.0064 uses the masses of the uranium atoms alone, but the gas that effuses is UF₆.",["The lighter molecule effuses slightly faster.","Graham: √(M_heavy/M_light) using the WHOLE molecule.","√(352.05/349.04)."]],
["m12-a-072",12,26,3,"Calculation","mcq","Under identical conditions, gas X takes twice as long as O₂ to effuse. What is the molar mass of X?",["128 g/mol","64.0 g/mol","16.0 g/mol","8.00 g/mol","45.3 g/mol"],0,"time ∝ √M, so t(X)/t(O₂) = 2 = √(M(X)/32.00) → M(X) = 32.00 × 2² = 128 g/mol. 64.0 forgets to square; 16.0 and 8.00 treat a slower gas as lighter.",["Slower to effuse means heavier.","Square the time ratio.","32.00 × 4."]],
["m12-a-073",12,26,4,"Exam","mcq","Cotton plugs soaked in NH₃ and in HCl are placed at opposite ends of a 100.0 cm glass tube. Where a white ring of NH₄Cl forms, the gases met. If each gas moved at a rate given by Graham’s law, how far from the NH₃ end would the ring form?",["31.8 cm","40.6 cm","50.0 cm","59.4 cm","68.2 cm"],3,"NH₃ (17.03) is lighter than HCl (36.46), so it travels farther: distance ratio = √(36.46/17.03) = 1.463. NH₃ covers 1.463/(1 + 1.463) of the tube = 59.4 cm from its end (the ring sits nearer the HCl end). 40.6 cm is the distance from the HCl end; the others skip the square root.",["Which gas is lighter, so travels farther in the same time?","Distances travelled are in the ratio of the rates, √(M₂/M₁).","NH₃ covers 1.463 parts out of 2.463."]],
["m12-a-074",12,26,4,"Exam","mcq","An unknown hydrocarbon gas effuses at 0.695 times the rate of N₂ under the same conditions. Which formula fits?",["C₂H₆","C₄H₁₀","C₃H₈","C₅H₁₂","C₄H₈"],1,"M = M(N₂)/(0.695)² = 28.02/0.4830 = 58.0 g/mol. C₄H₁₀ = 58.12 g/mol fits; C₄H₈ is 56.10, C₃H₈ 44.09, C₂H₆ 30.07, C₅H₁₂ 72.15.",["Square the rate ratio.","M(X) = 28.02 ÷ 0.695².","You need about 58 g/mol."]],
["m12-a-075",12,26,2,"Concept","mcq","How does the Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution of molecular speeds for a gas change when its temperature is raised?",["The peak moves to higher speed and the curve becomes taller and narrower","The peak moves to lower speed and the curve becomes broader","The area under the curve increases because there are more molecules","Every molecule ends up moving at the same, higher speed","The peak moves to higher speed and the curve becomes lower and broader"],4,"At higher T the most probable speed increases and the speeds spread out, so the curve flattens and broadens. The area under the curve represents the total number of molecules, which does not change - so if the curve spreads out its peak must drop. Molecules always have a RANGE of speeds.",["Hotter molecules move faster on average - which way does the peak shift?","The total number of molecules (the area) stays fixed.","Spreading out a fixed area makes the peak lower."]],
["m12-a-076",12,26,2,"Concept","mcq","Maxwell-Boltzmann speed distributions are drawn for He and Ar at the same temperature. Which statement is FALSE?",["The He curve peaks at a higher speed","The two curves are identical because the temperature is the same","The Ar curve is taller and narrower","Both gases have the same average kinetic energy","The area under each curve represents the total number of atoms"],1,"Same T means the same average KINETIC ENERGY, not the same speeds. The lighter He atoms move faster, so the He curve peaks at a higher speed and is broader and flatter; the Ar curve is taller and narrower. So \"identical curves\" is false.",["Does the same temperature mean the same speed?","Same average KE; lighter particles must be faster.","Lighter gas → peak at higher speed, flatter curve."]],
["m12-a-077",12,26,1,"Concept","mcq","Real gases deviate MOST from ideal behavior under which conditions?",["Low pressure and high temperature","High pressure and high temperature","Low pressure and low temperature","High pressure and low temperature","Deviations do not depend on P or T"],3,"At high pressure the molecules are crowded, so their own volume is no longer negligible; at low temperature they move slowly enough for attractions to matter. Both break KMT assumptions. Low P and high T is where gases are MOST ideal.",["Which two KMT assumptions fail for real gases?","Molecular volume matters when crowded; attractions matter when slow.","Crowded = high P; slow = low T."]],
["m12-a-078",12,26,1,"Concept","mcq","In the van der Waals equation (P + an²/V²)(V − nb) = nRT, what do the constants a and b correct for?",["a: attractions between molecules; b: the volume of the molecules themselves","a: the volume of the molecules; b: attractions between molecules","a: the speed of the molecules; b: their mass","a: elastic collisions; b: inelastic collisions","a: temperature errors; b: pressure-gauge errors"],0,"Attractions make molecules hit the walls less hard, so the measured P is lower than ideal; adding an²/V² corrects it upward. The molecules occupy real space, so the free volume is V − nb. Large a = strong IMFs; large b = big molecules.",["One term adds to P, the other subtracts from V.","Attractions lower the measured pressure; molecular size reduces the free volume.","a goes with attractions."]],
["m12-a-079",12,26,2,"Concept","mcq","Which gas would be expected to have the LARGEST van der Waals constant a?",["He","Ne","N₂","CH₄","H₂O"],4,"a measures intermolecular attraction. H₂O molecules hydrogen bond, by far the strongest attractions in the list (a ≈ 5.46 L²·atm/mol², versus 2.25 for CH₄, 1.39 for N₂ and 0.034 for He). The others have only dispersion forces.",["a reflects the forces between molecules.","Which gas has the strongest intermolecular forces?","Look for hydrogen bonding."]],
["m12-a-080",12,26,2,"Concept","mcq","Which gas would be expected to have the LARGEST van der Waals constant b?",["H₂","CH₄","C₄H₁₀","C₂H₆","C₃H₈"],2,"b is the excluded volume per mole - the size of the molecule. Butane, C₄H₁₀, is the largest molecule here (b ≈ 0.12 L/mol, versus about 0.027 for H₂).",["b is about molecular size.","Bigger molecules exclude more volume.","Pick the largest molecule."]],
["m12-a-081",12,26,4,"Calculation","mcq","For 1.00 mol of CO₂ in a 1.00 L container at 300. K, calculate the pressure from the van der Waals equation (a = 3.59 L²·atm/mol², b = 0.0427 L/mol).",["21.0 atm","24.6 atm","22.1 atm","25.7 atm","29.3 atm"],2,"P = nRT/(V − nb) − an²/V² = (1.00)(0.08206)(300.)/(1.00 − 0.0427) − (3.59)(1.00)²/(1.00)² = 25.72 − 3.59 = 22.1 atm. The ideal gas law gives 24.6 atm; the real pressure is lower because attractions dominate here. Adding the a-term (29.3) has its sign backwards; 25.7 includes only b and 21.0 only a.",["Solve the van der Waals equation for P.","P = nRT/(V − nb) − an²/V².","nRT/(V − nb) ≈ 25.7 atm."]],
["m12-a-082",12,26,4,"Calculation","num","Use the van der Waals equation to find the pressure of 2.00 mol of Cl₂ in a 4.00 L tank at 0 °C (a = 6.49 L²·atm/mol², b = 0.0562 L/mol). (Enter a number in atm.)",["9.91 atm"],9.908876170387897,"nRT = (2.00)(0.08206)(273.15) = 44.83 L·atm. V − nb = 4.00 − 2.00(0.0562) = 3.888 L. an²/V² = 6.49(4.00)/16.0 = 1.622 atm. P = 44.83/3.888 − 1.622 = 9.91 atm (the ideal gas law gives 11.2 atm). Remember n² in the a-term.",["P = nRT/(V − nb) − an²/V².","n² = 4.00, V² = 16.0; T = 273.15 K.","First term ≈ 11.53 atm; subtract ≈ 1.62 atm."]],
["m12-a-083",12,26,2,"Concept","mcq","At 1 atm and 300 K, which gas behaves MOST like an ideal gas?",["NH₃","H₂O","SO₂","He","CO₂"],3,"Helium atoms are tiny and have only very weak dispersion forces, so both van der Waals corrections are smallest. NH₃ and H₂O hydrogen bond, SO₂ is polar, and CO₂ is larger with stronger dispersion forces.",["Ideal means no attractions and negligible size.","Which particle is smallest and least attractive?","Think noble gas."]],
["m12-a-084",12,26,4,"Concept","mcq","For CH₄ at 300 K, PV/nRT is LESS than 1 at moderate pressures (around 100 atm) but GREATER than 1 at very high pressures (around 1000 atm). Which explanation is correct?",["At moderate pressure the molecules’ volume lowers PV; at very high pressure attractions raise it","At moderate pressure attractions lower the pressure; at very high pressure the molecules’ own volume dominates","The molecules slow down at high pressure, raising their kinetic energy","Collisions become inelastic at moderate pressure and elastic again at high pressure","The CH₄ molecules break apart at high pressure, increasing n"],1,"Attractions pull molecules back from the walls, making the real P (and PV) lower than ideal - this wins at moderate pressure. When the gas is squeezed hard, the molecules’ own volume means the real volume (and PV) is larger than ideal, so PV/nRT rises above 1. Swapping the two effects is the trap.",["Which correction lowers PV and which raises it?","Attractions lower P; excluded volume makes the real V larger than the ideal V.","Crowding matters most at the highest pressures."]],
["m12-a-085",12,26,2,"Concept","mcq","According to kinetic-molecular theory, why does the pressure of a gas increase when its volume is decreased at constant temperature?",["The molecules move faster in a smaller container","Each molecule hits the wall with more force","The molecules attract each other more strongly","The molecules themselves get smaller","The molecules strike the walls more often because they have less space to cross"],4,"At constant T the molecules keep the same average speed and kinetic energy. In a smaller volume they travel shorter distances between wall collisions, so collisions are more FREQUENT - and pressure is the force of those collisions per unit area. Faster molecules or harder hits require a higher temperature.",["Constant temperature means constant average speed.","Pressure comes from wall collisions.","What changes when the walls are closer together?"]],
["m12-a-086",12,26,3,"Calculation","mcq","The average kinetic energy of one mole of gas molecules is (3/2)RT. What is it at 25 °C?",["0.312 kJ","2.48 kJ","3.72 kJ","7.44 kJ","36.7 J"],2,"E = (3/2)(8.314 J/(mol·K))(298.15 K) = 3718 J = 3.72 kJ per mole - the same for ANY gas at 25 °C. Using 25 K, leaving out the 3/2, using R in L·atm, or doubling the 3/2 gives the other values.",["Energy needs R in joules.","R = 8.314 J/(mol·K), T = 298.15 K.","1.5 × 8.314 × 298.15, then convert J to kJ."]],
["m12-a-087",12,26,4,"Calculation","num","At what kelvin temperature would SO₂ molecules have the same rms speed as O₂ molecules at 300. K? (Enter a number in K.)",["601 K"],600.5625,"u(rms) = √(3RT/M) is the same when T/M is the same: T(SO₂) = 300. K × (64.06/32.00) = 601 K. The heavier molecule must be hotter to move as fast.",["Set the two rms expressions equal.","T₁/M₁ = T₂/M₂.","300 × 64.06/32.00."]],
["m12-a-088",12,26,1,"Concept","mcq","According to kinetic-molecular theory, what causes the pressure a gas exerts on its container?",["Collisions of the gas particles with the container walls","Attractions between the gas particles","The weight of the gas pressing down on the bottom","Repulsions between the gas particles at a distance","Heat flowing out through the container walls"],0,"Pressure is force per unit area, and in a gas the force comes from particles striking the walls. Ideal gas particles have no attractions or repulsions; the weight of a gas is negligible compared with collision forces.",["What do the particles do to the walls?","Force per area from many impacts.","Collisions."]],
["m12-a-089",12,26,3,"Concept","mcq","A sealed, rigid container of gas is heated. According to kinetic-molecular theory, which statement explains why its pressure rises?",["The molecules grow larger and fill more of the container","More molecules are formed as the temperature rises","The attractions between molecules increase","The molecules move faster but hit the walls less often","The molecules move faster, so they hit the walls both more often and harder"],4,"Average kinetic energy is proportional to T. Faster molecules collide with the walls more frequently AND transfer more momentum per collision, so the pressure rises (Gay-Lussac: P ∝ T at fixed n and V). The number and size of the molecules do not change.",["What does temperature measure at the molecular level?","Average KE ∝ T.","Two effects: frequency and force of collisions."]],
["m13-a-001",13,27,1,"Concept","mcq","Which statement about nuclear reactions is FALSE?",["They can change one element into another","Different isotopes of the same element can behave completely differently","Like chemical reactions, their rates rise sharply with temperature","They involve energy changes roughly a million times larger per atom than chemical reactions","They involve the nucleus rather than the outer electrons"],2,"Radioactive decay rates are unaffected by temperature, pressure or catalysts - a half-life is a property of the nucleus alone. All the other statements are true: nuclear reactions change Z (so the element), isotopes such as ²³⁵U and ²³⁸U behave very differently, and the energies are about 10⁶ times larger.",["Four statements are true differences between nuclear and chemical change.","Can you speed up radioactive decay by heating the sample?","Decay rate depends only on the nucleus."]],
["m13-a-002",13,27,1,"Concept","mcq","The term \"nucleon\" refers to:",["protons and neutrons","protons only","neutrons only","protons, neutrons and electrons","any particle emitted in radioactive decay"],0,"Nucleons are the particles in the nucleus: protons and neutrons. The mass number A counts nucleons. Electrons are outside the nucleus.",["Think \"nucleus\" + \"-on\".","What does the mass number count?","Both kinds of particle found in the nucleus."]],
["m13-a-003",13,27,1,"Concept","mcq","How many neutrons are in the nuclide ⁶⁰₂₇Co?",["27","60","87","33","30"],3,"N = A − Z = 60 − 27 = 33. 27 is the number of protons; 60 is the mass number (protons + neutrons); 87 adds instead of subtracting.",["The top number is the mass number, the bottom the atomic number.","N = A − Z.","60 − 27."]],
["m13-a-004",13,27,2,"Concept","mcq","Which pair of nuclides are isotopes of each other?",["¹⁴₆C and ¹⁴₇N","¹²₆C and ¹⁴₆C","⁴⁰₁₈Ar and ⁴⁰₂₀Ca","³₁H and ³₂He","¹⁶₈O and ¹⁸₉F"],1,"Isotopes have the same atomic number Z (same element) but different mass numbers. ¹²C and ¹⁴C both have Z = 6. The other pairs share a mass number (or neither number) but are different elements.",["Isotopes are the same element.","Same Z, different A.","Look for matching subscripts."]],
["m13-a-005",13,27,1,"Concept","mcq","An alpha particle is identical to:",["a high-speed electron, ⁰₋₁β","a positron, ⁰₊₁β","a high-energy photon, ⁰₀γ","a neutron, ¹₀n","a helium-4 nucleus, ⁴₂He"],4,"An α particle is two protons and two neutrons - a ⁴He nucleus with a 2+ charge. β⁻ is an electron, β⁺ a positron, γ a photon.",["Alpha has mass number 4.","It carries a 2+ charge.","Which nucleus has 2 protons and 2 neutrons?"]],
["m13-a-006",13,27,2,"Concept","mcq","When a nucleus emits an alpha particle, how do its mass number A and atomic number Z change?",["A decreases by 2, Z decreases by 4","A decreases by 4, Z decreases by 2","A unchanged, Z increases by 1","A decreases by 4, Z unchanged","A unchanged, Z decreases by 2"],1,"The nucleus loses ⁴₂He: two protons and two neutrons. So A − 4 and Z − 2 (the element moves two places to the left).",["What particle leaves?","α = ⁴₂He: subtract its top and bottom numbers.","A − 4, Z − 2."]],
["m13-a-007",13,27,2,"Concept","mcq","In beta (β⁻) decay, what happens inside the nucleus?",["A proton becomes a neutron and a positron is emitted, so Z decreases by 1","An inner electron is captured, so Z decreases by 1","Two protons and two neutrons are emitted, so Z decreases by 2","A neutron becomes a proton and an electron is emitted, so Z increases by 1","Only energy is emitted, so neither A nor Z changes"],3,"β⁻ decay: ¹₀n → ¹₁p + ⁰₋₁β. A stays the same and Z rises by one. The other choices describe positron emission (p → n, Z − 1), electron capture (Z − 1), alpha decay and gamma emission.",["β⁻ is an electron created in the nucleus.","Which nucleon can turn into a proton plus an electron?","n → p + e⁻, so Z goes up."]],
["m13-a-008",13,27,2,"Concept","mcq","Which type of decay changes neither the mass number nor the atomic number?",["Gamma emission","Alpha decay","Beta (β⁻) decay","Positron emission","Electron capture"],0,"A γ ray is a photon (⁰₀γ): it carries away energy from an excited nucleus but no mass number or charge. α lowers A and Z; β⁻ raises Z; positron emission and electron capture lower Z.",["Which emission is pure energy?","Its symbol has 0 on top and 0 on the bottom.","A photon."]],
["m13-a-009",13,27,4,"Trap","mcq","Which two decay modes produce the SAME daughter nuclide from a given parent?",["Alpha decay and beta (β⁻) decay","Beta (β⁻) decay and positron emission","Beta (β⁻) decay and electron capture","Alpha decay and gamma emission","Positron emission and electron capture"],4,"Positron emission (p → n + ⁰₊₁β) and electron capture (p + ⁰₋₁e → n) both turn one proton into one neutron: A unchanged, Z − 1. β⁻ goes the other way (Z + 1), which is why \"β⁻ and positron\" are opposites, not equal.",["Write the change in Z for each mode.","Which two modes both convert a proton into a neutron?","Both give Z − 1 with A unchanged."]],
["m13-a-010",13,27,2,"Calculation","mcq","Identify X in the nuclear equation: ²³⁸₉₂U → ⁴₂He + X",["²³⁴₉₁Pa","²⁴²₉₄Pu","²³⁴₉₀Th","²³⁴₉₂U","²³⁶₉₀Th"],2,"Balance the top and bottom numbers separately. A: 238 = 4 + A → A = 234. Z: 92 = 2 + Z → Z = 90, thorium. ²⁴²Pu added the α instead of removing it; ²³⁴Pa got Z wrong.",["Mass numbers must add up on both sides.","Subtract 4 from A and 2 from Z.","Z = 90 is thorium."]],
["m13-a-011",13,27,2,"Calculation","mcq","Identify X in the nuclear equation: ¹⁴₆C → X + ⁰₋₁β",["¹⁴₅B","¹⁰₄Be","¹⁴₇N","¹³₆C","¹⁵₇N"],2,"A: 14 = A + 0 → 14. Z: 6 = Z + (−1) → Z = 7, nitrogen. β⁻ emission RAISES Z. ¹⁴B is the common sign slip (6 − 1 instead of 6 + 1).",["The β particle has charge −1, so mind the sign.","6 = Z − 1.","Z = 7: nitrogen."]],
["m13-a-012",13,27,2,"Calculation","mcq","Identify X in the nuclear equation: ²²⁶₈₈Ra → ²²²₈₆Rn + X",["⁴₂He","⁰₋₁β","⁰₊₁β","¹₀n","⁰₀γ"],0,"Missing: A = 226 − 222 = 4 and Z = 88 − 86 = 2, which is ⁴₂He, an alpha particle.",["Find the missing A and Z by subtraction.","A = 4 and Z = 2.","That is a helium nucleus."]],
["m13-a-013",13,27,3,"Calculation","mcq","Identify X in the nuclear equation: ⁴⁰₁₉K + ⁰₋₁e → X",["⁴⁰₂₀Ca","³⁹₁₈Ar","⁴¹₁₉K","⁴⁰₁₈Ar","⁴⁰₁₉K"],3,"This is electron capture. A: 40 + 0 = 40. Z: 19 + (−1) = 18, argon. ⁴⁰Ca would come from β⁻ decay (Z + 1), which ⁴⁰K can also do - but the equation as written captures an electron.",["The electron is on the LEFT: it is captured, not emitted.","Add the bottom numbers: 19 + (−1).","Z = 18: argon."]],
["m13-a-014",13,27,2,"Calculation","mcq","Identify X in the nuclear equation: ¹³¹₅₃I → X + ⁰₋₁β",["¹³¹₅₂Te","¹³¹₅₄Xe","¹²⁷₅₁Sb","¹³⁰₅₃I","¹³²₅₄Xe"],1,"β⁻ decay: A unchanged (131), Z + 1 = 54, xenon. ¹³¹Te has the sign backwards.",["β⁻ keeps A and changes Z.","53 = Z − 1.","Z = 54: xenon."]],
["m13-a-015",13,27,2,"Calculation","mcq","Identify X in the nuclear equation: ¹⁸₉F → X + ⁰₊₁β",["¹⁸₁₀Ne","¹⁷₉F","¹⁴₇N","¹⁸₉F","¹⁸₈O"],4,"Positron emission: A unchanged (18); Z: 9 = Z + 1 → Z = 8, oxygen. ¹⁸F is the PET isotope; ¹⁸Ne would be β⁻ decay.",["The positron has charge +1.","9 = Z + 1.","Z = 8: oxygen."]],
["m13-a-016",13,27,2,"Calculation","mcq","Identify X in the nuclear equation: ²³⁹₉₄Pu → X + ⁴₂He",["²³⁵₉₃Np","²³⁵₉₂U","²⁴³₉₆Cm","²³⁷₉₂U","²³⁹₉₂U"],1,"A: 239 − 4 = 235. Z: 94 − 2 = 92, uranium.",["Alpha removes 4 from A and 2 from Z.","A = 235, Z = 92.","Uranium."]],
["m13-a-017",13,27,2,"Calculation","mcq","Identify X in the nuclear equation: ⁹⁰₃₈Sr → X + ⁰₋₁β",["⁹⁰₃₇Rb","⁸⁶₃₆Kr","⁸⁹₃₈Sr","⁹⁰₃₉Y","⁹¹₃₉Y"],3,"β⁻: A stays 90, Z = 38 + 1 = 39, yttrium.",["β⁻ raises Z by one.","A does not change.","Z = 39: yttrium."]],
["m13-a-018",13,27,2,"Calculation","mcq","Identify X in the nuclear equation: ²⁰₉F → ²⁰₁₀Ne + X",["⁰₋₁β","⁰₊₁β","⁴₂He","¹₀n","¹₁H"],0,"A: 20 = 20 + A → A = 0. Z: 9 = 10 + Z → Z = −1. A particle with A = 0, Z = −1 is a β⁻ particle (an electron). A positron would need Z = +1.",["Find A and Z of X by subtraction.","A = 0, Z = −1.","Mass number 0 and charge −1."]],
["m13-a-019",13,27,3,"Calculation","mcq","Identify X in the nuclear equation: ⁷₄Be + X → ⁷₃Li",["⁰₊₁β (positron)","¹₁H (proton)","¹₀n (neutron)","⁰₀γ (gamma photon)","⁰₋₁e (electron capture)"],4,"X is on the reactant side. A: 7 + A = 7 → A = 0. Z: 4 + Z = 3 → Z = −1. An electron absorbed by the nucleus = electron capture. A positron would have to be on the PRODUCT side to lower Z.",["X is being added to the nucleus, not emitted.","A = 0, Z = −1 on the left side.","An inner-shell electron is captured."]],
["m13-a-020",13,27,2,"Calculation","mcq","Identify X in the nuclear equation: ²¹⁰₈₄Po → ²⁰⁶₈₂Pb + X",["⁰₋₁β","⁰₊₁β","⁴₂He","¹₀n","²₁H"],2,"A: 210 − 206 = 4; Z: 84 − 82 = 2 → ⁴₂He, an α particle. Elements beyond Z = 83 typically decay by α.",["Subtract to get the missing numbers.","A = 4, Z = 2.","An alpha particle."]],
["m13-a-021",13,28,2,"Calculation","mcq","Identify X in the nuclear equation: ¹⁴₇N + ⁴₂He → ¹⁷₈O + X",["¹₀n (a neutron)","⁰₋₁β (a β⁻ particle)","¹₁H (a proton)","²₁H (a deuteron)","⁴₂He (an α particle)"],2,"Rutherford's first artificial transmutation (1919). A: 14 + 4 = 17 + A → A = 1. Z: 7 + 2 = 8 + Z → Z = 1. A = 1, Z = 1 is a proton.",["Add the reactants first.","A: 18 = 17 + A; Z: 9 = 8 + Z.","A = 1, Z = 1."]],
["m13-a-022",13,28,2,"Calculation","mcq","Identify X in the nuclear equation: ⁹₄Be + ⁴₂He → ¹²₆C + X",["¹₀n (a neutron)","¹₁H (a proton)","⁰₀γ (a gamma photon)","⁰₋₁β (a β⁻ particle)","²₁H (a deuteron)"],0,"Chadwick's discovery of the neutron. A: 9 + 4 = 12 + A → A = 1. Z: 4 + 2 = 6 + Z → Z = 0. A = 1, Z = 0 is a neutron.",["Sum the top numbers and the bottom numbers on the left.","A = 1, Z = 0.","A neutral particle of mass number 1."]],
["m13-a-023",13,28,2,"Calculation","mcq","Identify X in the nuclear equation: ²⁷₁₃Al + ⁴₂He → ³⁰₁₅P + X",["¹₁H","⁰₊₁β","⁰₋₁β","¹₀n","⁰₀γ"],3,"A: 27 + 4 = 30 + A → A = 1. Z: 13 + 2 = 15 + Z → Z = 0. X is a neutron. (The ³⁰P made this way was the first artificial radioactive isotope.)",["Balance A and Z separately.","A = 1; Z = 0.","Neutron."]],
["m13-a-024",13,28,3,"Calculation","mcq","Identify X in the nuclear equation: ²³⁵₉₂U + ¹₀n → ¹⁴¹₅₆Ba + ⁹²₃₆Kr + X",["2 ¹₀n","3 ¹₀n","¹₀n","4 ¹₀n","3 ¹₁H"],1,"Z: 92 + 0 = 56 + 36 = 92, so X carries no charge. A: 235 + 1 = 236; 141 + 92 = 233, so X must carry 3 mass units: 3 neutrons. Forgetting the incoming neutron gives 2.",["Do not forget the neutron on the LEFT.","Left A = 236; right so far = 233.","Three uncharged particles of A = 1."]],
["m13-a-025",13,28,3,"Calculation","mcq","Identify X in the nuclear equation: ²³⁵₉₂U + ¹₀n → ⁹⁴₃₈Sr + X + 2¹₀n",["¹⁴¹₅₄Xe","¹³⁹₅₄Xe","¹⁴⁰₅₆Ba","¹⁴²₅₄Xe","¹⁴⁰₅₄Xe"],4,"A: 235 + 1 = 236 = 94 + A + 2 → A = 140. Z: 92 = 38 + Z → Z = 54, xenon. ¹³⁹Xe forgets the incoming neutron, ¹⁴¹Xe counts only one product neutron, ¹⁴²Xe ignores both product neutrons, and ¹⁴⁰Ba has the wrong Z.",["Count all neutrons: 1 in, 2 out.","236 = 94 + A + 2.","A = 140, Z = 54."]],
["m13-a-026",13,28,2,"Calculation","mcq","Identify X in the nuclear equation: ²₁H + ³₁H → ⁴₂He + X",["¹₁H","¹₀n","⁰₋₁β","⁰₊₁β","²₁H"],1,"Deuterium-tritium fusion. A: 2 + 3 = 4 + A → A = 1. Z: 1 + 1 = 2 + Z → Z = 0. X is a neutron.",["Balance A and Z.","A = 1, Z = 0.","Neutral, mass number 1."]],
["m13-a-027",13,27,2,"Calculation","mcq","Identify X in the nuclear equation: ²⁴¹₉₅Am → ²³⁷₉₃Np + X",["⁰₋₁β","⁰₊₁β","¹₀n","⁴₂He","⁴₁H"],3,"Americium-241 (in smoke detectors) is an α emitter: A drops by 4, Z by 2.",["Subtract to find X.","A = 4, Z = 2.","α particle."]],
["m13-a-028",13,27,4,"Exam","mcq","²³²₉₀Th undergoes alpha decay, then β⁻ decay, then another β⁻ decay. What is the final nuclide?",["²²⁸₉₀Th","²²⁸₈₈Ra","²²⁸₉₂U","²²⁴₈₈Ra","²²⁸₈₉Ac"],0,"α: ²³²₉₀Th → ²²⁸₈₈Ra. β⁻: → ²²⁸₈₉Ac. β⁻: → ²²⁸₉₀Th. Net: A − 4 and Z back to 90 - an isotope of the starting element. ²²⁸Ra stops after the α, ²²⁸Ac after one β⁻, and ²²⁸U forgets that the α lowered Z by 2.",["Apply one step at a time.","α: A − 4, Z − 2; each β⁻: Z + 1.","Z: 90 − 2 + 1 + 1."]],
["m13-a-029",13,27,3,"Calculation","mcq","The uranium-238 decay series ends at lead-206 (²³⁸₉₂U → ²⁰⁶₈₂Pb). How many α and β⁻ particles are emitted in total?",["6 α and 8 β⁻","8 α and 10 β⁻","8 α and 2 β⁻","32 α and 10 β⁻","8 α and 6 β⁻"],4,"Only α changes A: (238 − 206)/4 = 8 α. Eight α lower Z by 16: 92 − 16 = 76. To reach Z = 82 you need 82 − 76 = 6 β⁻ (each raises Z by 1). 32 α forgets to divide by 4; 10 β⁻ subtracts the Z drop instead of making it up.",["Only α decay changes the mass number.","Number of α = ΔA ÷ 4; then fix Z with β⁻.","8 α take Z to 76."]],
["m13-a-030",13,27,3,"Calculation","mcq","Thorium-232 decays through a series to lead-208 (²³²₉₀Th → ²⁰⁸₈₂Pb). How many α and β⁻ particles are emitted?",["4 α and 6 β⁻","6 α and 8 β⁻","6 α and 4 β⁻","8 α and 6 β⁻","24 α and 4 β⁻"],2,"α count = (232 − 208)/4 = 6, which lowers Z by 12 to 78. Then 82 − 78 = 4 β⁻.",["ΔA comes only from α.","24 ÷ 4 = 6 α; Z drops to 78.","β⁻ count = 82 − 78."]],
["m13-a-031",13,27,4,"Calculation","mcq","Uranium-235 decays through a series to lead-207 (²³⁵₉₂U → ²⁰⁷₈₂Pb). How many α and β⁻ particles are emitted?",["7 α and 10 β⁻","4 α and 7 β⁻","7 α and 4 β⁻","8 α and 6 β⁻","7 α and 2 β⁻"],2,"α = (235 − 207)/4 = 7, lowering Z by 14 to 78. β⁻ = 82 − 78 = 4. \"8 α and 6 β⁻\" is the memorised ²³⁸U answer applied to the wrong series.",["Do not reuse the U-238 answer - compute.","α = (235 − 207) ÷ 4.","After 7 α, Z = 78."]],
["m13-a-032",13,27,4,"Trap","mcq","A nuclide emits one α particle followed by two β⁻ particles. Which statement describes the final nuclide?",["It is an isotope of the original element with a mass number 4 lower","It is the same nuclide as the original","It is an element two places lower with the same mass number","It is an element two places higher with a mass number 4 lower","It is an isotope of the original element with the same mass number"],0,"α: A − 4, Z − 2. Two β⁻: Z + 2, A unchanged. Net: Z unchanged (same element), A − 4. So it is an isotope of the parent, 4 mass units lighter (e.g. ²³⁸U → ²³⁴U).",["Track A and Z separately.","Only α changes A; the two β⁻ undo the α’s change in Z.","Net ΔZ = 0, ΔA = −4."]],
["m13-a-033",13,27,1,"Concept","mcq","For stable nuclides of the lighter elements (Z up to about 20), the neutron-to-proton ratio N/Z is approximately:",["0.5","1.5","2","1","3"],3,"The band of stability starts near N/Z = 1 (⁴He, ¹²C, ¹⁶O, ⁴⁰Ca) and rises to about 1.5 for the heaviest stable nuclei (²⁰⁶Pb has 124/82 ≈ 1.51), because extra neutrons are needed to offset proton-proton repulsion.",["Think of ¹²C or ¹⁶O.","Count neutrons and protons in ¹⁶₈O.","8 neutrons / 8 protons."]],
["m13-a-034",13,27,3,"Concept","mcq","Phosphorus-32 (stable phosphorus is phosphorus-31) is radioactive. Its most likely mode of decay is:",["positron emission","β⁻ emission","electron capture","α emission","neutron capture"],1,"³²P (15 p, 17 n) has one more neutron than the stable ³¹P (N/Z = 17/15 = 1.13 versus 1.07), so it lies ABOVE the band of stability - neutron-rich. β⁻ decay turns a neutron into a proton (³²P → ³²S), lowering N/Z. Positron emission and electron capture are for neutron-poor nuclides; α is for Z above 83.",["Compare the mass number with the stable isotope (or the atomic mass on the table).","Too many neutrons → which decay converts n into p?","Neutron-rich nuclides undergo β⁻ decay."]],
["m13-a-035",13,27,3,"Concept","mcq","Carbon-11 is radioactive (carbon’s atomic mass is 12.01). Its most likely mode of decay is:",["β⁻ emission","α emission","gamma emission only","neutron emission","positron emission"],4,"¹¹C has 6 p and 5 n (N/Z = 0.83), fewer neutrons than stable ¹²C - it is neutron-POOR, below the band. Positron emission (or electron capture) turns a proton into a neutron: ¹¹C → ¹¹B + ⁰₊₁β. β⁻ would make it even more neutron-poor.",["Is ¹¹C lighter or heavier than the stable isotope?","Neutron-poor nuclides need to turn p into n.","β⁺ (or electron capture)."]],
["m13-a-036",13,27,2,"Concept","mcq","Uranium-238 decays mainly by which mode?",["β⁻ emission","α emission","positron emission","electron capture","it is stable"],1,"No nuclide with Z above 83 is stable; heavy nuclei like ²³⁸U (Z = 92) shed mass and charge efficiently by α emission (²³⁸U → ²³⁴Th + ⁴He).",["What is unusual about Z = 92?","All nuclides with Z greater than 83 are radioactive.","Heavy nuclei lose ⁴He."]],
["m13-a-037",13,27,4,"Concept","mcq","Which nuclide is most likely to decay by POSITRON emission? (Atomic masses: O 16.00, H 1.008, Ra 226.0.)",["²⁰₈O","¹⁶₈O","³₁H","¹⁵₈O","²²⁶₈₈Ra"],3,"¹⁵O (8 p, 7 n) is lighter than stable ¹⁶O - neutron-poor - so it emits a positron (¹⁵O → ¹⁵N + β⁺); it is a PET isotope. ²⁰O and ³H are neutron-rich (β⁻); ¹⁶O is stable (doubly magic); ²²⁶Ra (Z = 88) emits α.",["Positron emitters are neutron-poor.","Compare each mass number with the atomic mass on the table.","Which oxygen isotope is lighter than 16?"]],
["m13-a-038",13,27,4,"Concept","mcq","Which nuclide is most likely to decay by β⁻ emission? (Atomic masses: Co 58.93, F 19.00, N 14.01, Fe 55.85, Po 209.)",["⁶⁰₂₇Co","¹⁸₉F","¹³₇N","⁵⁵₂₆Fe","²¹⁰₈₄Po"],0,"⁶⁰Co is heavier than the stable ⁵⁹Co - neutron-rich - so it undergoes β⁻ decay (⁶⁰Co → ⁶⁰Ni + β⁻, the cancer-therapy source). ¹⁸F, ¹³N and ⁵⁵Fe are lighter than their stable isotopes (neutron-poor: β⁺ or electron capture); ²¹⁰Po (Z = 84) emits α.",["β⁻ fixes a neutron excess.","Which nuclide has a mass number ABOVE its element’s atomic mass?","⁶⁰ versus 58.93."]],
["m13-a-039",13,27,2,"Concept","mcq","Which nuclide is \"doubly magic\" (a magic number of BOTH protons and neutrons)?",["²⁰⁷₈₂Pb","²⁰⁹₈₃Bi","¹⁹⁷₇₉Au","²³⁸₉₂U","²⁰⁸₈₂Pb"],4,"Magic numbers: 2, 8, 20, 28, 50, 82 (and 126 for neutrons). ²⁰⁸Pb has Z = 82 and N = 208 − 82 = 126 - both magic, making it exceptionally stable. ²⁰⁷Pb has N = 125.",["List the magic numbers.","Compute N = A − Z for each.","Z = 82 and N = 126."]],
["m13-a-040",13,27,2,"Concept","mcq","Which combination of proton and neutron numbers is MOST common among stable nuclides?",["Odd Z and odd N","Even Z and odd N","Even Z and even N","Odd Z and even N","All four combinations are about equally common"],2,"Nucleons pair up like electrons do, so even-even nuclides are by far the most numerous stable nuclides (about 157). Odd-odd stable nuclides are very rare (only ²H, ⁶Li, ¹⁰B, ¹⁴N and a few heavy ones).",["Nucleons, like electrons, are more stable when paired.","Pairing works for both protons and neutrons.","Both numbers even."]],
["m13-a-041",13,27,1,"Concept","mcq","Rank alpha, beta and gamma radiation in order of INCREASING penetrating power.",["γ < β < α","β < α < γ","α < β < γ","α < γ < β","γ < α < β"],2,"α particles are heavy and doubly charged, so they interact strongly and are stopped by paper or skin. β particles (electrons) go farther - a few mm of aluminum stops them. γ photons have no charge or mass and need thick lead or concrete.",["Bigger, more highly charged particles are stopped soonest.","Paper stops α; aluminum stops β; lead is needed for γ.","α is least penetrating."]],
["m13-a-042",13,27,2,"Concept","mcq","Which shielding is the MINIMUM needed to stop each type of radiation?",["α: a sheet of paper; β: a few mm of aluminum; γ: several cm of lead","α: several cm of lead; β: a few mm of aluminum; γ: a sheet of paper","α: a few mm of aluminum; β: a sheet of paper; γ: several cm of lead","All three are stopped by a sheet of paper","All three need several cm of lead"],0,"α (least penetrating) is stopped by paper or the outer layer of skin; β by a thin sheet of metal such as aluminum or by plastic; γ needs dense material - thick lead or concrete.",["Penetrating power: α < β < γ.","The weakest penetrator needs the thinnest shield.","Paper → aluminum → lead."]],
["m13-a-043",13,27,3,"Concept","mcq","Which statement about the biological hazard of radiation is correct?",["An α emitter is most dangerous outside the body, because α radiation penetrates deepest","γ rays are harmless because they have no mass and no charge","β particles cannot ionize atoms","An α emitter is most dangerous when inhaled or swallowed, because α particles deposit all their energy in a short path","Radiation damage comes only from heating the tissue"],3,"α particles are the most strongly IONIZING but least penetrating: outside the body your dead skin stops them, but inside (e.g. inhaled radon or polonium) they dump all their energy into nearby cells. γ rays are penetrating and do ionize. Damage comes from ionization - creating ions and free radicals - not heating.",["Separate \"penetrating\" from \"ionizing\".","α: highly ionizing, short range.","Inside the body nothing shields the cells."]],
["m13-a-044",13,27,2,"Concept","mcq","Which of the following is NOT ionizing radiation?",["X-rays","Radio waves","γ rays","α particles","β particles"],1,"Ionizing radiation carries enough energy to knock electrons out of atoms and molecules, forming ions and free radicals. α, β, γ and X-rays do. Radio waves are low-energy photons (long wavelength) - far too little energy per photon.",["Ionizing means energetic enough to remove an electron.","Photon energy ∝ 1/λ.","Which has the longest wavelength?"]],
["m13-a-045",13,27,3,"Concept","mcq","A beam containing α, β⁻ and γ radiation passes between two electrically charged plates. What happens?",["α bends toward the positive plate, β⁻ toward the negative plate, γ is undeflected","All three bend toward the positive plate","γ bends most because it travels fastest","None of them bend, because nuclear radiation has no charge","α bends toward the negative plate, β⁻ bends toward the positive plate (more sharply), γ is undeflected"],4,"α is positive (2+) so it is attracted to the negative plate; β⁻ is negative so it bends toward the positive plate, and much more sharply because an electron is about 7000 times lighter. γ photons are uncharged and go straight.",["Give each type its charge.","Opposite charges attract.","A lighter particle is deflected more easily."]],
["m13-a-046",13,28,1,"Concept","mcq","Which statement about the half-life of a radioactive nuclide is correct?",["It gets shorter as the sample gets smaller","It is the same no matter how much of the nuclide you start with","It doubles each time half of the sample decays","It can be shortened by heating the sample","It is the time for the whole sample to decay"],1,"Radioactive decay is first order, so t½ = 0.693/k is a constant independent of the amount present. Every half-life halves whatever is left; the sample never \"finishes\". Temperature has no effect on nuclear decay.",["Decay is first order.","t½ = 0.693/k contains no amount.","Constant."]],
["m13-a-047",13,28,2,"Calculation","mcq","What percentage of a radioactive sample remains after four half-lives?",["25.0%","12.5%","3.12%","6.25%","93.8%"],3,"(½)⁴ = 1/16 = 0.0625 = 6.25%. 12.5% is three half-lives, 3.12% five (3.125, with the exact 5 rounded to even); 93.8% is the fraction that has DECAYED.",["Each half-life multiplies by ½.","Fraction left = (½)ⁿ.","Compute (½)⁴."]],
["m13-a-048",13,28,2,"Calculation","mcq","Iodine-131 has a half-life of 8.02 days. How long does it take for 12.5% of a sample to remain?",["24.1 days","8.02 days","16.0 days","64.2 days","1.00 day"],0,"12.5% = 1/8 = (½)³, so three half-lives: 3 × 8.02 = 24.1 days. 64.2 days is 8 half-lives (confusing \"1/8\" with \"8 half-lives\").",["Write 12.5% as a fraction.","1/8 = (½)ⁿ - find n.","n = 3 half-lives."]],
["m13-a-049",13,28,2,"Calculation","num","Phosphorus-32 has a half-life of 14.3 days. What mass of a 80.0 mg sample remains after 57.2 days? (Enter a number in mg.)",["5.00 mg"],5,"57.2/14.3 = 4.00 half-lives. 80.0 mg × (½)⁴ = 80.0/16 = 5.00 mg.",["How many half-lives fit into 57.2 days?","Mass left = initial × (½)ⁿ.","80.0 ÷ 16."]],
["m13-a-050",13,28,3,"Calculation","mcq","Cobalt-60 has a half-life of 5.27 years. How much of a 1.00 g sample remains after 2.00 years?",["0.231 g","0.250 g","0.380 g","0.769 g","0.810 g"],3,"2.00 years is not a whole number of half-lives, so use first-order kinetics. k = 0.693/5.27 yr = 0.1315 yr⁻¹. N = N₀e^(−kt) = 1.00 g × e^(−0.1315 × 2.00) = 0.769 g. 0.810 g assumes the decay is linear; 0.250 g treats 2 years as 2 half-lives; 0.231 g is the amount that DECAYED.",["A fraction of a half-life: use ln(N/N₀) = −kt.","k = 0.693/t½.","N = e^(−0.1315 × 2.00)."]],
["m13-a-051",13,28,2,"Calculation","num","Strontium-90 has a half-life of 28.8 years. What is its first-order decay constant k? (Enter a number in yr⁻¹.)",["0.0241 yr⁻¹"],0.024062499999999997,"k = 0.693/t½ = 0.693/28.8 yr = 0.0241 yr⁻¹.",["Decay is first order.","k = 0.693/t½.","0.693 ÷ 28.8."]],
["m13-a-052",13,28,4,"Calculation","mcq","Strontium-90 has a half-life of 28.8 years. How long until only 10.0% of a sample remains?",["4.38 yr","41.6 yr","95.7 yr","144 yr","288 yr"],2,"ln(N/N₀) = −kt with k = 0.693/28.8 = 0.02406 yr⁻¹. ln(0.100) = −2.303, so t = 2.303/0.02406 = 95.7 yr (about 3.3 half-lives). 288 yr thinks 10% means ten half-lives; 41.6 yr used log instead of ln; 4.38 yr found when 10% has DECAYED.",["10.0% is not a power of ½ - use logarithms.","ln(0.100) = −kt.","t = 2.303 ÷ 0.02406 yr⁻¹."]],
["m13-a-053",13,28,3,"Calculation","num","The activity of a radioactive sample falls from 1200 counts/min to 850 counts/min in 10.0 hours. What is its half-life? (Enter a number in hours.)",["20.1 h"],20.09624819441105,"Activity is proportional to the number of nuclei, so ln(A/A₀) = −kt. k = ln(1200/850)/10.0 h = 0.3448/10.0 = 0.03448 h⁻¹. t½ = 0.693/k = 20.1 h.",["Activity drops by the same first-order law as the number of nuclei.","Find k from ln(A₀/A) = kt.","Then t½ = 0.693/k."]],
["m13-a-054",13,28,2,"Calculation","mcq","A wooden artifact has 25.0% of the carbon-14 activity of living wood. How old is it? (t½ of ¹⁴C = 5730 yr)",["5.73 × 10³ yr","1.15 × 10⁴ yr","1.43 × 10³ yr","2.29 × 10⁴ yr","4.30 × 10³ yr"],1,"25.0% = ¼ = (½)², so two half-lives: 2 × 5730 = 11,460 ≈ 1.15 × 10⁴ yr. 2.29 × 10⁴ yr is four half-lives (thinking \"¼ → 4\"); 4.30 × 10³ yr is 75% of 5730.",["Write 25% as a power of ½.","¼ = (½)².","Two half-lives."]],
["m13-a-055",13,28,4,"Calculation","mcq","Charcoal from an ancient hearth shows a ¹⁴C activity of 9.60 disintegrations per minute per gram of carbon; living wood shows 15.3 dpm/g. How old is the charcoal? (t½ of ¹⁴C = 5730 yr)",["1.67 × 10³ yr","2.13 × 10³ yr","2.67 × 10³ yr","3.60 × 10³ yr","3.85 × 10³ yr"],4,"k = 0.693/5730 yr = 1.209 × 10⁻⁴ yr⁻¹. t = ln(A₀/A)/k = ln(15.3/9.60)/1.209 × 10⁻⁴ = 0.4661/1.209 × 10⁻⁴ = 3.85 × 10³ yr. The traps: treating decay as linear, using log instead of ln, or forgetting to divide by 0.693.",["Activity is proportional to the ¹⁴C remaining.","t = ln(A₀/A) ÷ k with k = 0.693/5730.","ln(15.3/9.60) = 0.466."]],
["m13-a-056",13,28,4,"Calculation","num","A bone fragment contains 18.0% of the carbon-14 found in living bone. Estimate its age. (t½ of ¹⁴C = 5730 yr; enter a number in years.)",["1.42 × 10⁴ yr"],14178.63635348736,"k = 0.693/5730 = 1.209 × 10⁻⁴ yr⁻¹. t = ln(N₀/N)/k = ln(1/0.180)/1.209 × 10⁻⁴ = 1.715/1.209 × 10⁻⁴ = 1.42 × 10⁴ yr (about 2.5 half-lives).",["18.0% is not a neat power of ½.","ln(N/N₀) = −kt with N/N₀ = 0.180.","t = 1.715 ÷ 1.209 × 10⁻⁴ yr⁻¹."]],
["m13-a-057",13,28,2,"Concept","mcq","Why can radiocarbon dating be used only for objects that were once alive and are less than about 50,000 years old?",["Only living things contain any carbon at all","¹⁴C decays faster in older objects","Rocks contain only ¹⁴C, so their ratio never changes","Living things keep a constant ¹⁴C/¹²C ratio by exchanging carbon until death; after about 9 half-lives too little ¹⁴C is left to measure","After 50,000 years ¹⁴C turns back into ¹²C"],3,"While alive, an organism exchanges CO₂ (by photosynthesis or eating) and matches the atmosphere's ¹⁴C/¹²C ratio. At death the exchange stops and ¹⁴C decays with t½ = 5730 yr. After about 50,000 yr (≈ 9 half-lives, 0.2% left) the activity is too small to measure reliably. The half-life never changes.",["What sets the starting ¹⁴C level?","Exchange with the atmosphere stops at death.","How much is left after 9 half-lives?"]],
["m13-a-058",13,28,3,"Calculation","mcq","A 64.0 g sample of a radioactive isotope decays to 4.00 g in 36 days. What is its half-life?",["9.0 days","18 days","4.0 days","2.2 days","12 days"],0,"64.0 → 32.0 → 16.0 → 8.00 → 4.00 g is four halvings (64.0/4.00 = 16 = 2⁴). t½ = 36 days ÷ 4 = 9.0 days. 2.2 days divides 36 by the factor 16 (2.25, with the exact 5 rounded to even); 12 days counts 3 halvings.",["How many times do you halve 64 to reach 4?","64/4 = 16 = 2ⁿ.","36 days ÷ 4."]],
["m13-a-059",13,28,1,"Calculation","mcq","A 2.40 g sample of a radioactive nuclide decays for three half-lives. What mass of the nuclide remains?",["0.800 g","0.600 g","1.20 g","2.10 g","0.300 g"],4,"2.40 → 1.20 → 0.600 → 0.300 g. (½)³ = 1/8 of 2.40 g = 0.300 g. 0.800 g divides by 3; 2.10 g is how much decayed.",["Halve it once per half-life.","Three halvings.","2.40 ÷ 8."]],
["m13-a-060",13,28,4,"Trap","mcq","After exactly two half-lives, what percentage of the ORIGINAL radioactive nuclei has DECAYED?",["25%","50%","75%","100%","12.5%"],2,"After two half-lives (½)² = 25% remains, so 100 − 25 = 75% has decayed. 25% answers \"remains\" instead of \"decayed\"; 100% assumes two halves make a whole.",["Read the question: decayed, not remaining.","Remaining = (½)².","100% − 25%."]],
["m13-a-061",13,28,4,"Exam","mcq","Radium-226 (226.0 g/mol) has a half-life of 1600 years. What is the activity of 1.00 g of radium-226 in decays per second? (1 yr = 3.156 × 10⁷ s; activity = kN.)",["6.07 × 10⁻¹⁴ decays/s","1.83 × 10¹⁰ decays/s","3.66 × 10¹⁰ decays/s","5.28 × 10¹⁰ decays/s","1.15 × 10¹⁸ decays/s"],2,"k = 0.693/(1600 × 3.156 × 10⁷ s) = 1.372 × 10⁻¹¹ s⁻¹. N = (1.00 g / 226.0 g/mol)(6.022 × 10²³) = 2.665 × 10²¹ nuclei. Activity = kN = 3.66 × 10¹⁰ decays/s - about 1 curie, which is how the curie was defined. The traps: leaving k per YEAR (1.15 × 10¹⁸), forgetting Avogadro's number, or dividing N by t½ without the 0.693.",["Activity = kN, with k in s⁻¹ and N as a number of nuclei.","Convert the half-life to seconds; grams → moles → nuclei.","k ≈ 1.37 × 10⁻¹¹ s⁻¹; N ≈ 2.66 × 10²¹."]],
["m13-a-062",13,28,4,"Exam","mcq","A patient receives 25.0 mCi of technetium-99m (t½ = 6.01 h). Ignoring biological elimination, how long until the activity has fallen to 1.00 mCi?",["150. h","27.9 h","12.1 h","6.01 h","4.64 h"],1,"ln(A₀/A) = kt with k = 0.693/6.01 h = 0.1153 h⁻¹. t = ln(25.0)/0.1153 = 3.219/0.1153 = 27.9 h (about 4.6 half-lives). 4.64 h is the number of HALF-LIVES, not hours; 12.1 h used log instead of ln; 150. h multiplies the half-life by the ratio 25 instead of taking its logarithm.",["Activity follows first-order decay.","t = ln(A₀/A) ÷ k.","ln 25 = 3.22; k = 0.1153 h⁻¹."]],
["m13-a-063",13,28,4,"Exam","mcq","A rock contains 0.500 mol of ²⁰⁶Pb for every 1.000 mol of ²³⁸U. Assuming all the lead came from uranium decay (t½ of ²³⁸U = 4.47 × 10⁹ yr), how old is the rock?",["1.81 × 10⁹ yr","2.24 × 10⁹ yr","2.62 × 10⁹ yr","4.47 × 10⁹ yr","7.09 × 10⁹ yr"],2,"Each ²⁰⁶Pb came from one ²³⁸U, so originally there were 1.000 + 0.500 = 1.500 mol U. N/N₀ = 1.000/1.500. t = ln(N₀/N)/k = ln(1.500) × 4.47 × 10⁹/0.693 = 2.62 × 10⁹ yr. 4.47 × 10⁹ yr assumes half the uranium is gone (a Pb : U ratio of 1 : 1 would mean that); 1.81 × 10⁹ yr forgets the 0.693.",["How much uranium was there at the start?","N₀ = U now + Pb formed.","N/N₀ = 1.000/1.500; use ln."]],
["m13-a-064",13,28,1,"Concept","mcq","The mass defect of a nucleus is:",["the mass of the nucleus minus the mass of its separate protons and neutrons","the mass of its separate protons and neutrons minus the mass of the nucleus","the mass lost when the atom loses its electrons","the mass of the neutrons only","the difference between the mass number and the atomic number"],1,"A nucleus weighs LESS than its separated nucleons. That missing mass (Δm = Σ nucleons − nucleus, a positive number) is the mass equivalent of the binding energy, E = Δmc², released when the nucleus forms.",["Which is heavier: the nucleus or its free nucleons?","Δm is defined to be positive.","Separate nucleons minus the nucleus."]],
["m13-a-065",13,28,3,"Calculation","mcq","Calculate the binding energy of a helium-4 nucleus. Atomic masses: ⁴He 4.002603 amu, ¹H 1.007825 amu, neutron 1.008665 amu; 1 amu = 931.5 MeV.",["28.3 MeV","0.0304 MeV","7.07 MeV","1.91 × 10³ MeV","3.76 × 10³ MeV"],0,"Δm = 2(1.007825) + 2(1.008665) − 4.002603 = 0.030377 amu. Binding energy = 0.030377 × 931.5 MeV/amu = 28.3 MeV. 7.07 MeV is the energy PER NUCLEON; 0.0304 is the mass defect still in amu. Using ¹H atoms (not bare protons) makes the two electrons cancel with those in the ⁴He atom.",["Mass of the parts minus mass of the whole.","Δm = 2 m(¹H) + 2 m(n) − m(⁴He).","Multiply Δm in amu by 931.5."]],
["m13-a-066",13,28,3,"Calculation","num","Using the data for helium-4 (atomic mass 4.002603 amu; ¹H 1.007825 amu; neutron 1.008665 amu; 1 amu = 931.5 MeV), what is the binding energy PER NUCLEON? (Enter a number in MeV.)",["7.07 MeV"],7.074043875000126,"Δm = 0.030377 amu → binding energy = 28.30 MeV. ⁴He has 4 nucleons, so 28.30/4 = 7.07 MeV per nucleon.",["Find the total binding energy first.","Divide by the mass number A.","About 28.3 MeV ÷ 4."]],
["m13-a-067",13,28,4,"Calculation","num","Iron-56 (atomic mass 55.934939 amu) has 26 protons and 30 neutrons. With ¹H = 1.007825 amu, n = 1.008665 amu and 1 amu = 931.5 MeV, what is its binding energy per nucleon? (Enter a number in MeV.)",["8.79 MeV"],8.790382526785598,"Δm = 26(1.007825) + 30(1.008665) − 55.934939 = 0.528461 amu. Binding energy = 0.528461 × 931.5 = 492.3 MeV. Per nucleon: 492.3/56 = 8.79 MeV - the peak of the binding-energy curve.",["Mass defect: (26 × ¹H + 30 × n) − m(⁵⁶Fe).","Convert with 931.5 MeV/amu.","Divide the total by 56."]],
["m13-a-068",13,28,4,"Exam","mcq","The mass defect of ⁴He is 0.030377 g/mol. What is its binding energy in kJ per mole of nuclei? (E = Δmc², c = 2.998 × 10⁸ m/s)",["4.53 × 10⁻¹⁵ kJ/mol","9.11 kJ/mol","6.83 × 10⁸ kJ/mol","2.73 × 10⁹ kJ/mol","2.73 × 10¹² kJ/mol"],3,"Δm must be in kg: 0.030377 g = 3.0377 × 10⁻⁵ kg. E = (3.0377 × 10⁻⁵ kg)(2.998 × 10⁸ m/s)² = 2.730 × 10¹² J = 2.73 × 10⁹ kJ per mole. Leaving Δm in grams gives an answer 1000 times too big; forgetting to square c gives 9.11 kJ; dividing by Avogadro's number gives the energy of ONE nucleus. Compare: burning a mole of methane releases only 890 kJ.",["A joule is kg·m²/s² - convert grams to kg.","E = Δm c², then J → kJ.","3.0377 × 10⁻⁵ kg × 8.988 × 10¹⁶ m²/s²."]],
["m13-a-069",13,28,3,"Calculation","mcq","How much energy is released when one radium-226 nucleus α-decays: ²²⁶Ra → ²²²Rn + ⁴He? Atomic masses: ²²⁶Ra 226.025410, ²²²Rn 222.017578, ⁴He 4.002603 amu; 1 amu = 931.5 MeV.",["−4.87 MeV","0.00523 MeV","3.73 × 10³ MeV","4.87 × 10³ MeV","4.87 MeV"],4,"Δm = m(reactants) − m(products) = 226.025410 − (222.017578 + 4.002603) = 0.005229 amu. E = 0.005229 × 931.5 = 4.87 MeV released. 3.73 × 10³ MeV forgets the mass of the α particle.",["Products weigh less than reactants; the missing mass became energy.","Δm = 226.025410 − 222.017578 − 4.002603.","Multiply by 931.5 MeV/amu."]],
["m13-a-070",13,28,4,"Calculation","num","Deuterium-tritium fusion: ²H + ³H → ⁴He + ¹n. Masses: ²H 2.014102, ³H 3.016049, ⁴He 4.002603, n 1.008665 amu. How much energy is released per fusion event? (1 amu = 931.5 MeV; enter a number in MeV.)",["17.6 MeV"],17.589514500000398,"Δm = (2.014102 + 3.016049) − (4.002603 + 1.008665) = 0.018883 amu. E = 0.018883 × 931.5 = 17.6 MeV per event.",["Sum the reactant masses and the product masses.","Δm = reactants − products.","Δm ≈ 0.0189 amu."]],
["m13-a-071",13,28,4,"Exam","mcq","For the fission ²³⁵U + ¹n → ¹⁴¹Ba + ⁹²Kr + 3 ¹n, the masses are ²³⁵U 235.043930, ¹⁴¹Ba 140.914411, ⁹²Kr 91.926156 and n 1.008665 amu. How much energy is released per fission? (1 amu = 931.5 MeV)",["−766 MeV","0.186 MeV","0.734 MeV","173 MeV","2.05 × 10³ MeV"],3,"Δm = (235.043930 + 1.008665) − (140.914411 + 91.926156 + 3 × 1.008665) = 0.186033 amu. E = 0.186033 × 931.5 = 173 MeV. The wrong choices count only one neutron on the right, drop the incoming neutron, leave the answer in amu, or divide by the 236 nucleons. Fission releases about ten times more energy per event than D-T fusion, but far less per gram of fuel.",["Count every neutron on both sides.","Left: 236.052595 amu. Right: 140.914411 + 91.926156 + 3.025995.","Δm ≈ 0.186 amu."]],
["m13-a-072",13,28,2,"Calculation","mcq","If 1.00 g of matter were converted completely into energy, how much energy would be released? (c = 2.998 × 10⁸ m/s)",["8.99 × 10¹³ J","8.99 × 10¹⁶ J","3.00 × 10⁵ J","8.99 × 10¹⁰ J","3.00 × 10⁸ J"],0,"E = mc² = (1.00 × 10⁻³ kg)(2.998 × 10⁸ m/s)² = 8.99 × 10¹³ J. Using 1.00 kg gives 8.99 × 10¹⁶ J; forgetting to square c gives 3.00 × 10⁵ J.",["Mass must be in kilograms.","E = mc².","10⁻³ × 8.988 × 10¹⁶."]],
["m13-a-073",13,28,3,"Calculation","mcq","Burning 1 mol of CH₄ releases 890 kJ. What mass is \"lost\" (converted to energy) in this chemical reaction? (c = 2.998 × 10⁸ m/s)",["9.90 × 10⁻¹² g","9.90 × 10⁻⁹ g","2.97 g","16.0 g","8.00 × 10²⁵ g"],1,"Δm = E/c² = 8.90 × 10⁵ J ÷ (2.998 × 10⁸ m/s)² = 9.90 × 10⁻¹² kg = 9.90 × 10⁻⁹ g - far too small to weigh, which is why mass is \"conserved\" in chemical reactions. The traps: reporting the kg value as grams, not squaring c, multiplying by c² instead of dividing, or confusing the energy with the 16.0 g of methane burned.",["E = Δmc², solve for Δm.","E must be in joules; Δm comes out in kg.","8.90 × 10⁵ ÷ 8.988 × 10¹⁶, then kg → g."]],
["m13-a-074",13,28,1,"Concept","mcq","Which description of nuclear fission is correct?",["Two light nuclei join into a heavier nucleus, releasing energy","A heavy nucleus splits into two medium-mass nuclei plus neutrons, releasing energy","A nucleus emits an α particle","A nucleus captures an electron from its inner shell","A medium-mass nucleus such as ⁵⁶Fe splits, releasing a large amount of energy"],1,"Fission splits a heavy nucleus (e.g. ²³⁵U after absorbing a neutron) into two lighter fragments and 2-3 neutrons. Joining light nuclei is fusion. ⁵⁶Fe sits at the top of the binding-energy curve, so splitting it would ABSORB energy.",["\"Fission\" means splitting.","Which nuclei release energy when split?","Heavy ones like uranium."]],
["m13-a-075",13,28,2,"Concept","mcq","Binding energy per nucleon is highest for nuclides near ⁵⁶Fe. Which conclusion follows?",["All nuclei release energy by fusion","All nuclei release energy by fission","Iron can release more energy by fusion than hydrogen can","Heavy nuclei release energy by fusion and light nuclei by fission","Very heavy nuclei release energy by fission and very light nuclei release energy by fusion"],4,"Energy is released whenever the products have a HIGHER binding energy per nucleon. Moving toward ⁵⁶Fe from either side does that: heavy nuclei split (fission), light nuclei join (fusion). Iron itself cannot release energy either way - which is why it accumulates in dying stars.",["Energy is released when nucleons become more tightly bound.","Both sides of the curve move toward the peak.","Heavy → split; light → join."]],
["m13-a-076",13,28,2,"Concept","mcq","In a nuclear fission chain reaction, what is the critical mass?",["The mass of uranium that releases 1 MeV","The minimum mass of fissionable material in which, on average, at least one neutron from each fission causes another fission","The mass lost as energy in one fission event","The maximum mass of fuel a reactor can hold safely","The mass of a neutron"],1,"Each fission releases 2-3 neutrons. In a small piece too many escape through the surface; at the critical mass enough are captured to keep the reaction going (one new fission per fission). Above it (supercritical) the rate grows rapidly.",["Think about neutrons escaping from a small sample.","A chain reaction must sustain itself.","One neutron per fission must go on to cause another."]],
["m13-a-077",13,28,2,"Concept","mcq","In a nuclear power reactor, what are the jobs of the moderator and the control rods?",["Moderator absorbs neutrons; control rods slow them down","Moderator cools the turbine; control rods produce neutrons","Both speed up neutrons so they can split ²³⁸U","Moderator (water or graphite) slows the neutrons; control rods (cadmium or boron) absorb neutrons to control the rate","Moderator stores the spent fuel; control rods contain the uranium fuel"],3,"Slow (thermal) neutrons are captured by ²³⁵U far more efficiently, so a moderator such as water or graphite slows them. Control rods of Cd or B absorb neutrons; pushing them in lowers the rate, pulling them out raises it. A separate coolant carries heat to make steam.",["Which part speeds things up and which slows the reaction?","²³⁵U captures SLOW neutrons best.","Control rods soak up neutrons."]],
["m13-a-078",13,28,2,"Concept","mcq","Why does nuclear fusion require temperatures of millions of kelvin?",["The positively charged nuclei must move fast enough to overcome their electrostatic repulsion","Fusion is endothermic and must absorb heat","Heat is needed to remove the neutrons from the nuclei","High temperature turns the nuclei into neutrons","Heat speeds up radioactive decay"],0,"Nuclei are positive and repel each other. Only at enormous temperatures (as in the Sun's core) do they collide hard enough to get close enough for the strong nuclear force to take over. Fusion of light nuclei is strongly EXOTHERMIC once it happens.",["What charge do nuclei carry?","Like charges repel.","They need very high kinetic energy to get close."]],
["m13-a-079",13,28,1,"Concept","mcq","Positron emission tomography (PET) uses isotopes such as ¹⁸F. What is actually detected?",["The positrons themselves as they leave the body","α particles emitted by the isotope","Neutrons released by the isotope","Radio waves emitted by the fluorine nucleus","Two γ photons travelling in opposite directions, produced when a positron annihilates an electron"],4,"The emitted positron travels a millimetre or so, meets an electron and both annihilate into two 511 keV γ photons flying in opposite directions. Detectors around the patient record the pairs and locate where the tracer (e.g. ¹⁸F-glucose) has gathered.",["A positron is antimatter.","Positron + electron → energy.","The energy leaves as a pair of photons."]],
["m13-a-080",13,28,2,"Concept","mcq","Why can a radioactive isotope be used as a tracer to follow an element through a living organism?",["Radioactive atoms move faster than stable atoms","Radioactive atoms react only with specific organs","A radioisotope behaves chemically like the stable isotopes of the element, but its radiation reveals where it goes","The radiation makes the organism absorb more of the element","Radioactive atoms cannot be metabolized, so they stay put"],2,"Chemistry depends on electrons, which are the same for every isotope of an element. So ¹³¹I is taken up by the thyroid exactly like ordinary iodine, and its emissions show where it went. Tracers follow the normal path; they do not change it.",["What determines how an atom reacts chemically?","Isotopes have identical electron arrangements.","Same chemistry, plus a signal."]],
["m13-a-081",13,28,3,"Concept","mcq","Which isotope is best suited for diagnostic imaging inside the body?",["Pu-239: α emitter, t½ = 24,100 yr","C-14: β⁻ emitter, t½ = 5730 yr","Tc-99m: γ emitter, t½ = 6.0 h","Sr-90: β⁻ emitter, t½ = 28.8 yr","Rn-222: α emitter, t½ = 3.8 d"],2,"An imaging isotope needs radiation that escapes the body to reach a detector (γ, penetrating) and a short half-life so the patient's dose ends quickly. ⁹⁹ᵐTc has both. α emitters damage tissue and cannot be seen from outside; long half-lives keep irradiating the patient.",["The radiation must get OUT of the body.","The dose should not linger.","Penetrating radiation + short half-life."]],
["m13-a-082",13,27,2,"Calculation","num","Uranium-238 (Z = 92) emits an α particle. How many neutrons are in the daughter nucleus? (Enter a whole number.)",["144"],144,"²³⁸₉₂U → ⁴₂He + ²³⁴₉₀Th. The daughter has A = 234 and Z = 90, so N = 234 − 90 = 144. (The parent had 146; α removes 2 neutrons.)",["Write the daughter’s A and Z first.","α: A − 4, Z − 2.","N = 234 − 90."],{"abs":0.5}],
["m13-a-083",13,27,2,"Calculation","num","Carbon-14 (Z = 6) undergoes β⁻ decay. How many protons are in the daughter nucleus? (Enter a whole number.)",["7"],7,"¹⁴₆C → ¹⁴₇N + ⁰₋₁β. A neutron turns into a proton, so the daughter has 6 + 1 = 7 protons (nitrogen).",["β⁻ changes a neutron into a proton.","Z goes up by 1.","6 + 1 protons."],{"abs":0.5}],
["m13-a-084",13,28,2,"Calculation","num","What percentage of a radioactive sample remains after five half-lives? (Enter a number in %.)",["3.12 %"],3.125,"(½)⁵ = 1/32 = 0.03125 = 3.125%, which is 3.12% to three sig figs - this course rounds an exact 5 to the even digit (Lecture 1).",["Multiply by ½ once per half-life.","Compute (½)⁵.","1/32 as a percent."]],
["m13-a-085",13,28,2,"Calculation","num","Iodine-131 has a half-life of 8.02 days. How many days does it take a 1.00 mg sample to decay to 0.125 mg? (Enter a number in days.)",["24.1 days"],24.06510990515481,"1.00 → 0.500 → 0.250 → 0.125 mg is three half-lives: 3 × 8.02 = 24.1 days.",["What fraction of the sample remains?","0.125 = (½)ⁿ.","n = 3 half-lives."]],
["m13-a-086",13,28,4,"Calculation","num","Radon-222 has a half-life of 3.82 days. What mass remains from a 5.00 g sample after 10.0 days? (Enter a number in g.)",["0.815 g"],0.8148932033024252,"k = 0.693/3.82 d = 0.1814 d⁻¹. N = 5.00 g × e^(−0.1814 × 10.0) = 5.00 × 0.1630 = 0.815 g. (10.0 days is 2.62 half-lives, so the answer must fall between 1.25 g and 0.625 g.)",["10.0 days is not a whole number of half-lives.","N = N₀e^(−kt), k = 0.693/t½.","kt ≈ 1.81."],{"tol":0.02}],
["m13-a-087",13,28,3,"Calculation","num","Cobalt-60 has a half-life of 5.27 yr. How long does it take for a cobalt-60 source to fall to 30.0% of its original activity? (Enter a number in years.)",["9.16 yr"],9.15575278325784,"k = 0.693/5.27 = 0.1315 yr⁻¹. t = ln(1/0.300)/k = 1.204/0.1315 = 9.16 yr.",["ln(A/A₀) = −kt.","A/A₀ = 0.300; k = 0.693/5.27.","ln(0.300) = −1.204."]],
["m13-a-088",13,28,4,"Exam","mcq","A 1.00 mg sample of phosphorus-32 (32.0 g/mol, t½ = 14.3 days) is stored for 28.6 days. How many ³²P atoms DECAYED in that time?",["4.70 × 10¹⁸ atoms","9.41 × 10¹⁸ atoms","1.41 × 10¹⁹ atoms","4.52 × 10²⁰ atoms","1.41 × 10²² atoms"],2,"28.6 days = 2 half-lives, so ¼ remains and ¾ decayed. Atoms at the start = (1.00 × 10⁻³ g ÷ 32.0 g/mol)(6.022 × 10²³) = 1.882 × 10¹⁹. Decayed = 0.750 × that = 1.41 × 10¹⁹ atoms. The traps: counting the atoms that REMAIN (¼), leaving mg as g, skipping the molar mass, or using one half-life.",["How many half-lives is 28.6 days?","Decayed fraction = 1 − (½)ⁿ; then mg → g → mol → atoms.","¾ of 1.88 × 10¹⁹ atoms."]],
["m13-a-089",13,28,4,"Exam","mcq","Samples of isotope A (t½ = 2.0 h) and isotope B (t½ = 6.0 h) start with equal numbers of nuclei. After 12.0 h, what is the ratio N(A)/N(B)?",["0.333","3.00","16.0","0.500","0.0625"],4,"In 12.0 h, A goes through 6 half-lives: (½)⁶ = 1/64. B goes through 2: (½)² = 1/4. N(A)/N(B) = (1/64)/(1/4) = 1/16 = 0.0625. 0.333 is the ratio of the half-lives (a linear assumption); 16.0 is the ratio upside down.",["Count half-lives separately for each isotope.","A: 6 half-lives; B: 2 half-lives.","(½)⁶ ÷ (½)²."]],
["m13-a-090",13,28,4,"Calculation","num","Each fission of a uranium-235 nucleus releases about 173.3 MeV. How much energy is released by the complete fission of 1.00 g of ²³⁵U (235.04 g/mol)? (1 MeV = 1.602 × 10⁻¹³ J; enter a number in kJ.)",["7.11 × 10⁷ kJ"],71131185.5513955,"Nuclei = (1.00 g ÷ 235.04 g/mol)(6.022 × 10²³) = 2.562 × 10²¹. Energy = 2.562 × 10²¹ × 173.3 MeV × 1.602 × 10⁻¹³ J/MeV = 7.113 × 10¹⁰ J = 7.11 × 10⁷ kJ - about what burning 2.5 tonnes of coal gives.",["grams → moles → nuclei.","Multiply by MeV per fission, then MeV → J → kJ.","About 2.56 × 10²¹ nuclei."]],
["m13-a-091",13,28,3,"Trap","mcq","Which statement about nuclear binding energy is FALSE?",["Binding energy is the energy needed to break a nucleus into its separate nucleons","Binding energy per nucleon is a measure of nuclear stability","A nucleus has MORE mass than the protons and neutrons that make it up","The mass defect and the binding energy are related by E = Δmc²","⁵⁶Fe has one of the highest binding energies per nucleon"],2,"A nucleus has LESS mass than its separated nucleons - the difference (mass defect) was released as binding energy when it formed. All the other statements are true.",["Forming a nucleus releases energy.","Released energy carries mass away (E = mc²).","So is the nucleus lighter or heavier than its parts?"]],
["m13-a-092",13,27,3,"Concept","mcq","A nuclide lies ABOVE the band of stability (its N/Z ratio is too high). Which decay process would bring it closer to the band?",["β⁻ emission, which lowers N by 1 and raises Z by 1","Positron emission, which raises N by 1 and lowers Z by 1","Electron capture, which raises N by 1 and lowers Z by 1","Gamma emission, which lowers N/Z directly","Neutron capture, which raises N by 1"],0,"Too many neutrons: convert a neutron into a proton. β⁻ decay does exactly that (n → p + e⁻), lowering N and raising Z, so N/Z drops. Positron emission and electron capture do the opposite and suit neutron-poor nuclides; γ changes neither N nor Z; neutron capture makes things worse.",["\"Above the band\" means neutron-rich.","You need fewer neutrons and more protons.","Which decay turns n into p?"]],
["m93-a-001",93,20,3,"Exam","mcq","SO₂ boils at −10 °C, while CO₂ sublimes at −78 °C. Which explanation, based on their Lewis structures, is correct?",["CO₂ has polar bonds but SO₂ does not","SO₂ is linear and CO₂ is bent","SO₂ is bent and polar, so it has dipole-dipole forces as well as dispersion; CO₂ is linear and nonpolar","SO₂ molecules hydrogen bond to one another","SO₂ has stronger covalent bonds, which must break when it boils"],2,"Lewis structures: CO₂ has two electron groups on C (two double bonds) → linear, and the two C=O dipoles cancel. SO₂ has three electron groups on S (two bonds + one lone pair) → bent, so its S−O dipoles do not cancel. A polar molecule adds dipole-dipole attractions, raising the boiling point. Boiling breaks IMFs, never covalent bonds, and neither molecule has an H to hydrogen bond with.",["Draw both Lewis structures and count electron groups on the central atom.","Does the lone pair on S change the shape?","Bent and polar beats linear and nonpolar."]],
["m93-a-002",93,19,4,"Exam","mcq","Which statement correctly compares BF₃ and NF₃?",["BF₃ is trigonal planar and nonpolar; NF₃ is trigonal pyramidal and polar","Both are trigonal planar and nonpolar","Both are trigonal pyramidal and polar","BF₃ is trigonal pyramidal and polar; NF₃ is trigonal planar and nonpolar","BF₃ is trigonal planar and polar; NF₃ is trigonal pyramidal and nonpolar"],0,"BF₃: B has 3 valence electrons, makes three bonds and has no lone pair (an electron-deficient octet exception) → 3 electron groups, trigonal planar; the three B−F dipoles cancel → nonpolar. NF₃: N has 5 valence electrons → three bonds + one lone pair → 4 electron groups, trigonal pyramidal → the dipoles do not cancel → polar. Same formula type, different shape.",["Count valence electrons on B and on N.","A lone pair on the central atom changes the molecular shape.","Planar AX₃ cancels; pyramidal AX₃ does not."]],
["m93-a-003",93,20,4,"Trap","mcq","CH₂Cl₂ is polar and CCl₄ is nonpolar, yet CCl₄ boils at 77 °C and CH₂Cl₂ at 40 °C. Which explanation is correct?",["CCl₄ molecules hydrogen bond to each other","CCl₄ is actually polar because its C−Cl bonds are polar","Boiling CCl₄ requires breaking C−Cl covalent bonds","CCl₄ is larger with more electrons, so its dispersion forces outweigh the dipole-dipole forces of CH₂Cl₂","CH₂Cl₂ hydrogen bonds, and hydrogen bonding lowers the boiling point"],3,"Polarity is not the whole story. CCl₄ (153.8 g/mol, 74 electrons) is far more polarizable than CH₂Cl₂ (84.9 g/mol, 42 electrons), so its London dispersion forces are larger than CH₂Cl₂'s dispersion + dipole-dipole forces combined. CCl₄ is tetrahedral and symmetric, so its bond dipoles cancel; neither compound can hydrogen bond (no H on N, O or F).",["Dispersion forces grow with the number of electrons.","Compare molar masses: 153.8 versus 84.9 g/mol.","A big enough nonpolar molecule can out-boil a small polar one."]],
["m93-a-004",93,18,3,"Exam","mcq","What are the molecular shape and polarity of XeF₄?",["Tetrahedral, nonpolar","Square planar, nonpolar","Seesaw, polar","Square pyramidal, polar","Octahedral, polar"],1,"Xe has 8 valence electrons; four bonds to F use 4, leaving 2 lone pairs: 6 electron groups (octahedral arrangement). The two lone pairs sit opposite each other, so the four F atoms form a square plane. The Xe−F dipoles cancel in pairs → nonpolar.",["Count electron groups on Xe: bonds + lone pairs.","6 groups with 2 lone pairs.","Lone pairs go opposite each other (trans)."]],
["m93-a-005",93,18,3,"Exam","mcq","What are the molecular shape and polarity of SF₄?",["Tetrahedral, nonpolar","Square planar, nonpolar","Trigonal pyramidal, polar","Seesaw, nonpolar","Seesaw, polar"],4,"S has 6 valence electrons: four S−F bonds + one lone pair = 5 electron groups (trigonal bipyramidal). The lone pair takes an equatorial position, giving a seesaw. The shape is not symmetric, so the bond dipoles do not cancel → polar.",["Count S’s valence electrons and its bonds.","5 electron groups, 1 lone pair.","Lone pair equatorial → seesaw."]],
["m93-a-006",93,18,3,"Exam","mcq","What is the molecular shape of ClF₃, and is it polar?",["Trigonal planar, nonpolar","T-shaped, polar","Trigonal pyramidal, polar","T-shaped, nonpolar","Seesaw, polar"],1,"Cl has 7 valence electrons: three Cl−F bonds + two lone pairs = 5 electron groups. Both lone pairs go equatorial, leaving the three F atoms in a T. The dipoles do not cancel → polar. Writing \"trigonal planar\" ignores the lone pairs.",["Cl has 7 valence electrons.","3 bonds + 2 lone pairs = 5 groups.","Two equatorial lone pairs leave a T."]],
["m93-a-007",93,18,2,"Exam","mcq","What is the molecular shape of the triiodide ion, I₃⁻?",["Bent","Trigonal planar","T-shaped","Linear","Trigonal pyramidal"],3,"Total valence electrons = 3(7) + 1 = 22. The central I has two bonds and three lone pairs = 5 electron groups (trigonal bipyramidal). All three lone pairs occupy equatorial positions, leaving the two bonded I atoms at the axial positions, 180° apart: linear.",["Count 22 valence electrons and place them.","The central I has 2 bonds and 3 lone pairs.","Three equatorial lone pairs → axial atoms in a line."]],
["m93-a-008",93,20,3,"Exam","mcq","Propane (CH₃CH₂CH₃, 44.1 g/mol), dimethyl ether (CH₃OCH₃, 46.1 g/mol) and ethanol (CH₃CH₂OH, 46.1 g/mol) have nearly equal molar masses. Rank them in order of INCREASING boiling point.",["propane < dimethyl ether < ethanol","ethanol < dimethyl ether < propane","dimethyl ether < propane < ethanol","propane < ethanol < dimethyl ether","they are all about equal because their molar masses are equal"],0,"With similar dispersion forces, the other IMFs decide. Propane: nonpolar, dispersion only (bp −42 °C). Dimethyl ether: bent at O, polar → dipole-dipole, but no O−H so no hydrogen bonding between its molecules (bp −24 °C). Ethanol: O−H → hydrogen bonding (bp 78 °C).",["Same mass means similar dispersion forces.","Which ones are polar? Which have an H bonded to O?","Dispersion only < dipole-dipole < hydrogen bonding."]],
["m93-a-009",93,20,3,"Exam","mcq","Rank H₂O, H₂S, H₂Se and H₂Te in order of INCREASING boiling point.",["H₂O < H₂S < H₂Se < H₂Te","H₂Te < H₂Se < H₂S < H₂O","H₂S < H₂O < H₂Se < H₂Te","H₂O < H₂Te < H₂Se < H₂S","H₂S < H₂Se < H₂Te < H₂O"],4,"For H₂S, H₂Se and H₂Te, dispersion forces grow with size, so the boiling point rises down the group (−60, −41, −2 °C). H₂O breaks the trend: it hydrogen bonds strongly, so it boils at 100 °C - highest of all despite being the lightest. \"H₂O lowest\" follows molar mass alone and forgets hydrogen bonding.",["Which of these can hydrogen bond?","For the others, bigger means stronger dispersion.","Water is the anomaly."]],
["m93-a-010",93,15,2,"Exam","mcq","Rank the lattice energies of MgO, NaCl, KBr and NaF from SMALLEST to LARGEST.",["MgO < NaF < NaCl < KBr","NaF < NaCl < KBr < MgO","KBr < NaCl < NaF < MgO","KBr < NaF < NaCl < MgO","NaCl < KBr < NaF < MgO"],2,"Lattice energy ∝ (q₁q₂)/r. MgO has 2+ and 2− ions, so it is by far the largest (~3800 kJ/mol). Among the 1+/1− salts, smaller ions give larger lattice energies: NaF (923) > NaCl (787) > KBr (671).",["Coulomb’s law: charge product over distance.","Charge matters most; then size.","Doubly charged ions first; then smallest ions."]],
["m93-a-011",93,15,2,"Concept","mcq","Which substance has the HIGHEST melting point?",["NaCl","KBr","MgO","I₂","CO₂"],2,"Ionic solids melt when the lattice is broken, and MgO (2+/2− ions, small) has the largest lattice energy, melting near 2800 °C. NaCl (801 °C) and KBr (734 °C) have smaller lattice energies; I₂ and CO₂ are molecular solids held only by dispersion forces.",["Ionic or molecular?","Among ionic solids, look at charge and size.","Highest ion charges."]],
["m93-a-012",93,15,1,"Concept","mcq","The lattice energy of an ionic compound, as defined in Silberberg, is:",["the energy needed to separate one mole of the solid into gaseous ions - always positive","the energy released when the solid dissolves in water","the energy needed to melt one mole of the solid","the energy released when gaseous atoms form the solid","always negative, because ionic solids are stable"],0,"ΔH°lattice is the enthalpy change for MX(s) → M⁺(g) + X⁻(g), which always ABSORBS energy (positive). The reverse step, forming the lattice from gaseous ions, releases the same amount.",["Think \"taking the lattice apart\".","Separating opposite charges costs energy.","Solid → gaseous ions, ΔH > 0."]],
["m93-a-013",93,20,1,"Concept","mcq","What is the strongest intermolecular force between methanol (CH₃OH) molecules?",["London dispersion forces","Ion-dipole forces","Covalent bonding","Hydrogen bonding","Ionic bonding"],3,"Methanol has an H bonded directly to O, and O lone pairs on neighbouring molecules - the requirement for hydrogen bonding. Dispersion forces are present too but weaker. Covalent and ionic bonds are INTRAmolecular, not between molecules.",["Look for H attached to N, O or F.","The O−H group.","An especially strong dipole-dipole force."]],
["m93-a-014",93,20,3,"Exam","mcq","Which molecule is polar AND can hydrogen bond with other molecules of itself?",["CH₃F","NH₃","CH₃OCH₃","CO₂","H₂S"],1,"NH₃ is trigonal pyramidal (polar) and has H atoms bonded to N, which can bond to the lone pair of a neighbouring N. CH₃F and CH₃OCH₃ are polar but their H atoms are bonded to C, so they cannot hydrogen bond with themselves. H₂S is polar but S is not electronegative enough. CO₂ is nonpolar.",["Two tests: polarity, and an H on N, O or F.","C−H never hydrogen bonds.","Which molecule has N−H bonds?"]],
["m93-a-015",93,19,3,"Exam","mcq","Which AX₃ molecule has NO net dipole moment?",["NH₃","PCl₃","ClF₃","NF₃","SO₃"],4,"SO₃: all six of S’s valence electrons are in bonds, so S has no lone pair (whether SO₃ is drawn with three S=O bonds or as resonance forms with one S=O and two S−O) → trigonal planar, symmetric, dipoles cancel → nonpolar. NH₃, PCl₃ and NF₃ have a lone pair (trigonal pyramidal); ClF₃ has two lone pairs (T-shaped). All four are polar.",["An AX₃ molecule is nonpolar only if it is trigonal planar.","Which central atom has no lone pair?","Count S’s electrons in SO₃."]],
["m93-a-016",93,19,4,"Exam","mcq","Which molecule is NONPOLAR even though all of its bonds are polar, so that only dispersion forces act between its molecules?",["SF₄","PCl₅","NH₃","CHCl₃","SO₂"],1,"PCl₅: 5 bonding groups, no lone pair → trigonal bipyramidal, and the dipoles cancel → nonpolar, so dispersion only. SF₄ (seesaw), NH₃ (pyramidal), CHCl₃ (tetrahedral but with different atoms) and SO₂ (bent) are all polar and add dipole-dipole forces (and NH₃ hydrogen bonds).",["Look for a symmetric shape with identical outer atoms.","No lone pairs on the central atom.","Five identical bonds around P."]],
["m93-a-017",93,19,2,"Concept","mcq","Which molecule has the SMALLEST bond angle around its central atom?",["CH₄","NH₃","BF₃","H₂O","CO₂"],3,"All of CH₄, NH₃ and H₂O have four electron groups, but lone pairs repel more than bonding pairs and squeeze the angle: CH₄ 109.5°, NH₃ 107°, H₂O 104.5° (two lone pairs). BF₃ is 120° and CO₂ is 180°.",["Start from the electron-group geometry.","Each lone pair compresses the bond angles.","Two lone pairs squeeze the most."]],
["m93-a-018",93,17,2,"Calculation","mcq","In a Lewis structure of SO₄²⁻ drawn with four S−O SINGLE bonds (each O with three lone pairs), what is the formal charge on sulfur?",["+2","0","−2","+1","+6"],0,"FC = valence e⁻ − (lone-pair e⁻ + ½ bonding e⁻) = 6 − (0 + ½ × 8) = +2. Each O is 6 − (6 + 1) = −1. Check: +2 + 4(−1) = −2, the ion charge. (Expanding S’s octet with two S=O bonds brings S to 0, which is why that structure is often preferred.)",["FC = V − (L + B/2).","S has 6 valence electrons, no lone pairs and 8 bonding electrons.","FC = 6 − 4."]],
["m93-a-019",93,17,3,"Exam","mcq","Rank the carbon-oxygen bond lengths in CO, CO₂, CO₃²⁻ and CH₃OH from SHORTEST to LONGEST.",["CH₃OH < CO₃²⁻ < CO₂ < CO","CO₂ < CO < CO₃²⁻ < CH₃OH","CO < CO₃²⁻ < CO₂ < CH₃OH","CO₃²⁻ < CO < CO₂ < CH₃OH","CO < CO₂ < CO₃²⁻ < CH₃OH"],4,"Higher bond order = shorter bond. CO has a triple bond (order 3); CO₂ two double bonds (order 2); CO₃²⁻ is a resonance hybrid of one double and two single bonds (order 4/3); CH₃OH has a C−O single bond (order 1).",["Find the C−O bond order in each.","Carbonate has three resonance structures.","Order: 3, 2, 4/3, 1."]],
["m93-a-020",93,16,1,"Concept","mcq","Which nitrogen-nitrogen bond is both the SHORTEST and the STRONGEST?",["The N=N bond in N₂H₂","The N−N bond in N₂H₄","The N≡N bond in N₂","They are all the same length","The N−N bond, because single bonds are strongest"],2,"Bond order 3 > 2 > 1: more shared pairs pull the atoms closer and take more energy to break. N≡N (110 pm, 945 kJ/mol) beats N=N (122 pm, 418) and N−N (146 pm, 160).",["Count shared electron pairs.","Higher bond order → shorter and stronger.","Triple bond."]],
["m93-a-021",93,16,4,"Exam","mcq","Use bond energies to estimate ΔH for CH₄(g) + 2O₂(g) → CO₂(g) + 2H₂O(g). Bond energies (kJ/mol): C−H 413, O=O 498, C=O (in CO₂) 799, O−H 467.",["+818 kJ","−802 kJ","−818 kJ","−3466 kJ","+2648 kJ"],2,"ΔH ≈ Σ(bonds broken) − Σ(bonds formed). Broken: 4 C−H + 2 O=O = 4(413) + 2(498) = 2648 kJ. Formed: 2 C=O + 4 O−H = 2(799) + 4(467) = 3466 kJ. ΔH ≈ 2648 − 3466 = −818 kJ. The ΔH°f route gives −802 kJ; bond energies are averages, so the estimate differs a little - but the question asked for the bond-energy estimate. +818 reverses the order (formed − broken).",["Bonds broken cost energy (+); bonds formed release it (−).","Count: 4 C−H and 2 O=O broken; 2 C=O and 4 O−H formed.","ΔH = 2648 − 3466."]],
["m93-a-022",93,16,2,"Concept","mcq","ΔH calculated from bond energies usually differs slightly from ΔH calculated from standard enthalpies of formation. Why?",["Bond energies are AVERAGE values over many different molecules","Bond energies include the energy of the electrons in lone pairs","ΔH°f values are only estimates, while bond energies are exact","Bond energies are measured for liquids, not gases","The two methods use opposite sign conventions, so they can never agree"],0,"A C−H bond in CH₄ is not exactly the same strength as one in CH₃OH; tables list averages, so a bond-energy ΔH is an estimate. ΔH°f values are measured for the actual compounds and are more accurate. Bond energies apply to the GAS phase.",["Is every C−H bond identical in strength?","Tables give one number for each bond type.","Averages → estimates."]],
["m93-a-023",93,16,3,"Calculation","mcq","Using bond energies H−H 432, Cl−Cl 243 and H−Cl 427 kJ/mol, estimate ΔH for H₂(g) + Cl₂(g) → 2HCl(g).",["+179 kJ","−854 kJ","+248 kJ","−179 kJ","−184.6 kJ"],3,"Broken: H−H + Cl−Cl = 432 + 243 = 675 kJ. Formed: 2 H−Cl = 2(427) = 854 kJ. ΔH ≈ 675 − 854 = −179 kJ. Forgetting the 2 on HCl gives +248 kJ; reversing the subtraction gives +179. (−184.6 kJ is 2 × ΔH°f(HCl) - close, but not the bond-energy estimate.)",["Bonds broken minus bonds formed.","Two H−Cl bonds form.","675 − 854."]],
["m93-a-024",93,15,4,"Exam","mcq","Use a Born-Haber cycle to find the lattice energy of NaCl. Data (kJ/mol): ΔH°f NaCl(s) = −411.1; Na(s) → Na(g) +107.8; IE₁ of Na +496; ½Cl₂(g) → Cl(g) +121.0; electron affinity of Cl −349.",["−787 kJ/mol","+787 kJ/mol","+1.48 × 10³ kJ/mol","+908 kJ/mol","+35.3 kJ/mol"],1,"Hess: ΔH°f = (atomize Na) + IE + (atomize Cl) + EA + (lattice-forming step). The four steps sum to 107.8 + 496 + 121.0 − 349 = 375.8 kJ. Lattice-forming step = −411.1 − 375.8 = −786.9 kJ, so the lattice energy (separating the solid) = +787 kJ/mol. Using +349 for the EA, using 242 kJ (all of Cl₂) instead of 121.0, or simply adding ΔH°f to the steps gives the other choices.",["The formation reaction equals the sum of the five steps.","The electron affinity is negative; only ½ Cl₂ is atomized.","Lattice step = −411.1 − 375.8."]],
["m93-a-025",93,21,3,"Calculation","mcq","Ethanol has a vapor pressure of 100. torr at 34.9 °C and ΔH(vap) = 38.6 kJ/mol. Estimate its vapor pressure at 63.5 °C.",["27.8 torr","1.00 × 10² torr","109 torr","182 torr","360. torr"],4,"ln(P₂/P₁) = −(ΔH/R)(1/T₂ − 1/T₁) with ΔH = 38,600 J/mol, T₁ = 308.05 K, T₂ = 336.65 K: ln(P₂/100.) = −(38600/8.314)(−2.758 × 10⁻⁴) = 1.280. P₂ = 100. × e^1.280 = 360. torr. A sign error gives 27.8 torr (heating must RAISE the vapor pressure); leaving ΔH in kJ leaves P almost unchanged; scaling P with T is not how vapor pressure behaves.",["Vapor pressure rises steeply (exponentially) with T.","Clausius-Clapeyron with ΔH in J/mol and T in K.","ln(P₂/P₁) ≈ +1.28."]],
["m93-a-026",93,21,4,"Calculation","num","A liquid has a vapor pressure of 115 torr at 25.0 °C and 487 torr at 60.0 °C. Calculate its enthalpy of vaporization. (Enter a number in kJ/mol.)",["34.1 kJ/mol"],34.05515379759536,"ΔH(vap) = R ln(P₂/P₁)/(1/T₁ − 1/T₂) = (8.314)(ln 4.235)/(1/298.15 − 1/333.15) = (8.314)(1.443)/(3.524 × 10⁻⁴) = 3.406 × 10⁴ J/mol = 34.1 kJ/mol.",["Rearrange Clausius-Clapeyron for ΔH.","Use kelvin; the answer comes out in J/mol.","ΔH = 8.314 × 1.443 ÷ 3.524 × 10⁻⁴."]],
["m93-a-027",93,21,4,"Exam","mcq","Water boils at 100.0 °C at 1.00 atm, and ΔH(vap) = 40.7 kJ/mol. At what temperature does water boil on a mountain where the pressure is 0.800 atm?",["25.4 °C","80.0 °C","93.8 °C","100.0 °C","106.5 °C"],2,"A liquid boils when its vapor pressure equals the outside pressure. 1/T₂ = 1/T₁ − (R/ΔH) ln(P₂/P₁) = 1/373.15 − (8.314/40700) ln(0.800) = 2.7255 × 10⁻³ K⁻¹, so T₂ = 366.9 K = 93.8 °C. A sign slip gives a boiling point ABOVE 100 °C; scaling the °C or kelvin temperature by 0.800 is not how vapor pressure works; and the boiling point does depend on pressure.",["Boiling point = temperature where vapor pressure = outside pressure.","Clausius-Clapeyron from (373.15 K, 1.00 atm) to (T₂, 0.800 atm).","ln(0.800) = −0.223."]],
["m93-a-028",93,21,3,"Calculation","mcq","How much heat is needed to turn 25.0 g of ice at −10.0 °C into liquid water at 25.0 °C? (c(ice) = 2.09 J/(g·°C), c(water) = 4.184 J/(g·°C), ΔH(fus) = 6.02 kJ/mol)",["3.14 kJ","3.29 kJ","8.35 kJ","11.0 kJ","11.5 kJ"],4,"Three steps. Warm ice: q = 25.0 × 2.09 × 10.0 = 522 J. Melt: (25.0/18.02 mol) × 6020 J/mol = 8352 J. Warm water: 25.0 × 4.184 × 25.0 = 2615 J. Total = 1.149 × 10⁴ J = 11.5 kJ. Skipping the melting step or the ice-warming step, or multiplying ΔH(fus) by grams instead of moles, gives the other choices.",["Sketch the heating curve: slope, plateau, slope.","q = mcΔT on slopes; q = nΔH(fus) on the plateau.","The melting step is the largest: about 8.35 kJ."]],
["m93-a-029",93,21,1,"Concept","mcq","On a phase diagram, what is true above the critical point?",["Solid, liquid and gas coexist in equilibrium","The substance can only exist as a solid","The vapor pressure becomes zero","The liquid boils at any pressure","The liquid and gas phases become indistinguishable (a supercritical fluid)"],4,"Beyond the critical temperature and pressure no amount of pressure can liquefy the gas: liquid and gas merge into one supercritical fluid. All three phases coexist at the TRIPLE point, a different point.",["Do not confuse the critical point with the triple point.","The liquid-gas line ends at the critical point.","Liquid and gas become one phase."]],
["m93-a-030",93,21,2,"Concept","mcq","Rank water, ethanol and diethyl ether in order of INCREASING vapor pressure at 25 °C.",["diethyl ether < ethanol < water","ethanol < water < diethyl ether","water < ethanol < diethyl ether","water < diethyl ether < ethanol","all three are equal at the same temperature"],2,"Stronger IMFs hold molecules in the liquid and LOWER the vapor pressure. Water has the most hydrogen bonding (two O−H per molecule); ethanol has one O−H; diethyl ether is polar but cannot hydrogen bond with itself, so it evaporates most easily.",["Vapor pressure goes the opposite way to IMF strength.","Count O−H groups available for hydrogen bonding.","Ether has none."]],
["m93-a-031",93,22,1,"Concept","mcq","How many atoms are in one face-centered cubic (FCC) unit cell?",["1","2","4","8","14"],2,"8 corners × ⅛ = 1, plus 6 faces × ½ = 3: a total of 4 atoms. 14 counts every atom touching the cell without sharing; 2 is body-centered cubic; 1 is simple cubic.",["Corner atoms are shared by 8 cells, face atoms by 2.","8 × ⅛ + 6 × ½.","1 + 3 atoms."]],
["m93-a-032",93,22,3,"Calculation","mcq","Copper crystallizes in a face-centered cubic lattice with an edge length of 361.5 pm. Calculate the density of copper.",["8.94 g/cm³","2.23 g/cm³","4.47 g/cm³","17.9 g/cm³","8.94 × 10⁶ g/cm³"],0,"Mass of one cell = 4 atoms × 63.55 g/mol ÷ 6.022 × 10²³ = 4.221 × 10⁻²² g. Edge = 361.5 pm = 3.615 × 10⁻⁸ cm, so V = (3.615 × 10⁻⁸ cm)³ = 4.724 × 10⁻²³ cm³. d = 4.221 × 10⁻²²/4.724 × 10⁻²³ = 8.94 g/cm³. Using 2 or 1 atoms per cell (BCC, simple cubic) or 8 gives the other densities; a pm → cm slip gives an absurd 10⁶.",["Density = mass of one cell ÷ volume of one cell.","FCC has 4 atoms; 1 pm = 10⁻¹⁰ cm.","V = (3.615 × 10⁻⁸ cm)³."]],
["m93-a-033",93,22,4,"Calculation","num","Iron crystallizes in a body-centered cubic lattice and has a density of 7.874 g/cm³. Calculate the atomic radius of iron. (Enter a number in pm.)",["124 pm"],124.12891897769354,"BCC has 2 atoms per cell. V = mass/density = (2 × 55.85/6.022 × 10²³ g)/(7.874 g/cm³) = 2.356 × 10⁻²³ cm³, so a = ∛V = 2.867 × 10⁻⁸ cm = 286.7 pm. In BCC the atoms touch along the body diagonal: 4r = √3·a, so r = √3 × 286.7/4 = 124 pm.",["Work backwards from density to the cell volume.","BCC: 2 atoms per cell and 4r = √3·a.","a ≈ 287 pm."]],
["m93-a-034",93,22,3,"Concept","mcq","Quartz (SiO₂) is very hard, does not conduct electricity, and melts above 1600 °C. What type of solid is it?",["Molecular","Network covalent","Ionic","Metallic","Atomic"],1,"In quartz every Si is covalently bonded to four O atoms in a continuous 3-D network; melting requires breaking covalent bonds, hence the high melting point and hardness. It does not conduct (no mobile ions or electrons). Molecular solids such as dry ice (CO₂) are soft and melt low.",["SiO₂ is not made of separate SiO₂ molecules.","What must break for it to melt?","Covalent bonds throughout the crystal."]],
["m93-a-035",93,24,4,"Exam","mcq","Dissolving 1.50 g of a nonelectrolyte in 25.0 g of benzene lowers the freezing point by 1.38 °C. K(f) of benzene = 5.12 °C/m. What is the molar mass of the solute?",["0.223 g/mol","80.9 g/mol","111 g/mol","223 g/mol","445 g/mol"],3,"m = ΔT/K(f) = 1.38/5.12 = 0.2695 mol/kg. Moles = m × kg solvent = 0.2695 × 0.0250 kg = 6.738 × 10⁻³ mol. M = 1.50 g ÷ 6.738 × 10⁻³ mol = 223 g/mol. Using 25.0 kg instead of 0.0250 kg, water's K(f) (1.86), or i = 2 for a nonelectrolyte gives the other choices.",["ΔT(f) → molality → moles → molar mass.","Molality uses KILOGRAMS of solvent: 0.0250 kg.","m = 0.2695 mol/kg."]],
["m93-a-036",93,24,4,"Calculation","num","A solution of 0.500 g of a protein in enough water to make 100.0 mL has an osmotic pressure of 2.65 torr at 25 °C. What is the molar mass of the protein? (Enter a number in g/mol.)",["3.51 × 10⁴ g/mol"],35083.5917735849,"Π = MRT (i = 1). Π = 2.65/760 = 3.487 × 10⁻³ atm. M = Π/RT = 3.487 × 10⁻³/(0.08206 × 298.15) = 1.425 × 10⁻⁴ mol/L. Moles in 0.1000 L = 1.425 × 10⁻⁵ mol. Molar mass = 0.500 g ÷ 1.425 × 10⁻⁵ mol = 3.51 × 10⁴ g/mol.",["Osmotic pressure gives molarity: M = Π/RT.","Π must be in atm to match R = 0.08206.","Then moles = M × 0.1000 L."]],
["m93-a-037",93,24,3,"Calculation","mcq","What is the freezing point of a 0.150 m aqueous solution of CaCl₂? (K(f) of water = 1.86 °C/m; assume complete dissociation.)",["−0.837 °C","−0.558 °C","−0.279 °C","−0.230 °C","+0.837 °C"],0,"CaCl₂ → Ca²⁺ + 2Cl⁻, so i = 3. ΔT(f) = iK(f)m = 3 × 1.86 × 0.150 = 0.837 °C. The freezing point is 0.000 − 0.837 = −0.837 °C. i = 1 or 2, a positive sign (freezing points go DOWN), or K(b) instead of K(f) gives the other choices.",["How many ions does one CaCl₂ make?","ΔT(f) = iK(f)m, and the freezing point is lowered.","i = 3 for CaCl₂."]],
["m93-a-038",93,24,3,"Exam","mcq","Which aqueous solution has the LOWEST freezing point? (Assume complete dissociation of electrolytes.)",["0.10 m CaCl₂","0.10 m NaCl","0.20 m glucose","0.12 m KNO₃","0.050 m Al₂(SO₄)₃"],0,"ΔT(f) ∝ i·m. CaCl₂: 3 × 0.10 = 0.30. Al₂(SO₄)₃: 5 × 0.050 = 0.25. KNO₃: 2 × 0.12 = 0.24. NaCl: 2 × 0.10 = 0.20. Glucose: 1 × 0.20 = 0.20. The largest i·m (CaCl₂) gives the biggest drop. Al₂(SO₄)₃ has the largest i, but its molality is only half.",["Colligative: count particles, not formula units.","Compute i × m for each.","Largest i·m → lowest freezing point."]],
["m93-a-039",93,24,3,"Calculation","mcq","What is the vapor pressure at 25 °C of a solution of 50.0 g of glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆, 180.16 g/mol) in 500.0 g of water? (P° of water at 25 °C = 23.76 torr)",["0.235 torr","21.6 torr","23.3 torr","23.5 torr","23.8 torr"],3,"Raoult: P = X(water)·P°. n(glucose) = 50.0/180.16 = 0.2775 mol; n(water) = 500.0/18.02 = 27.75 mol. X(water) = 27.75/28.02 = 0.9901. P = 0.9901 × 23.76 = 23.5 torr. 0.235 torr is the LOWERING, not the vapor pressure; using mass fractions or i = 2 (glucose does not dissociate) gives the others.",["Raoult’s law uses the mole fraction of the SOLVENT.","Convert both masses to moles.","X(water) ≈ 0.990."]],
["m93-a-040",93,24,3,"Calculation","num","By how many degrees does the boiling point of water rise when 25.0 g of NaCl is dissolved in 500. g of water? (K(b) = 0.512 °C/m; assume complete dissociation. Enter a number in °C.)",["0.876 °C"],0.8761122518822724,"m = (25.0/58.44 mol)/0.500 kg = 0.8556 mol/kg. NaCl gives i = 2. ΔT(b) = iK(b)m = 2 × 0.512 × 0.8556 = 0.876 °C, so the solution boils at about 100.88 °C.",["Molality first: mol NaCl per kg water.","i = 2 for NaCl.","ΔT(b) = 2 × 0.512 × 0.856."]],
["m93-a-041",93,24,4,"Calculation","num","What is the freezing point of a solution of 10.0 g of ethylene glycol (C₂H₆O₂, a nonelectrolyte) in 100.0 g of water? (K(f) = 1.86 °C/m; enter a number in °C.)",["−3.00 °C"],-2.996713282206612,"M(C₂H₆O₂) = 62.07 g/mol, so n = 0.1611 mol and m = 0.1611/0.1000 kg = 1.611 m. ΔT(f) = 1.86 × 1.611 = 3.00 °C. The freezing point is 0.00 − 3.00 = −3.00 °C. Entering +3.00 gives the depression, not the freezing point.",["Is the answer the change or the new freezing point?","m = mol solute / kg water; i = 1.","The freezing point is negative."]],
["m93-a-042",93,23,2,"Concept","mcq","Which substance is MOST soluble in hexane, C₆H₁₄?",["CCl₄","CH₃OH","NaCl","glycerol, C₃H₅(OH)₃","acetic acid, CH₃COOH"],0,"Like dissolves like: hexane is nonpolar, held together by dispersion forces, and dissolves nonpolar CCl₄. Methanol, glycerol and acetic acid hydrogen bond and prefer polar solvents; NaCl needs ion-dipole forces from a polar solvent such as water.",["Is hexane polar or nonpolar?","Like dissolves like.","Pick the nonpolar solute."]],
["m93-a-043",93,23,3,"Exam","mcq","Two DNA double helices have the same length. Helix A is 60% G-C base pairs; helix B is 40% G-C. Which one needs the higher temperature to separate its strands, and why?",["B, because A-T pairs are held by three hydrogen bonds","A, because G-C pairs are joined by covalent bonds","B, because it has more base pairs","A, because each G-C pair is held by three hydrogen bonds versus two for A-T","They are the same, because both have the same length"],3,"The two strands are held together by hydrogen bonds between complementary bases (plus base stacking). G-C pairs have three hydrogen bonds and A-T pairs two, so the G-C-rich helix A needs more energy to separate. Base pairs are not covalently bonded to each other.",["What holds the two strands together?","Count hydrogen bonds per base pair.","G-C: 3, A-T: 2."]],
["m93-a-044",93,23,2,"Concept","mcq","The α-helix and β-pleated sheet (protein secondary structure) are held together mainly by:",["covalent disulfide bonds between cysteine side chains","hydrogen bonds between backbone N−H and C=O groups","ionic bonds between charged side chains","peptide bonds between neighbouring amino acids","dispersion forces between nonpolar side chains"],1,"Secondary structure comes from regular hydrogen bonding along the backbone: the N−H of one peptide group to the C=O of another. Disulfide bridges, salt bridges and dispersion forces between side chains shape the TERTIARY structure; peptide bonds define the primary sequence.",["Secondary structure involves the backbone, not the side chains.","Which backbone groups can hydrogen bond?","N−H ··· O=C."]],
["m93-a-045",93,23,3,"Exam","mcq","In a folded protein, the side chain of lysine (−CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₂NH₃⁺) lies next to the side chain of aspartate (−CH₂COO⁻). Which interaction holds them together most strongly?",["A disulfide bond","London dispersion forces only","A peptide bond","A covalent C−N bond","An ionic attraction (salt bridge) between NH₃⁺ and COO⁻"],4,"At body pH lysine’s side chain carries NH₃⁺ and aspartate’s carries COO⁻; the opposite charges attract (a salt bridge), one of the tertiary-structure interactions. Disulfide bonds need two cysteines; peptide bonds join backbone amino and carboxyl groups.",["Look at the charges on the two side chains.","Opposite charges attract.","Salt bridge."]],
["m93-a-046",93,23,2,"Concept","mcq","Glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆) dissolves easily in water, but cholesterol (C₂₇H₄₆O) barely dissolves. Why?",["Cholesterol is ionic and glucose is covalent","Glucose has five O−H groups that hydrogen bond with water; cholesterol has one O−H on a large nonpolar hydrocarbon skeleton","Glucose has the larger molar mass","Cholesterol hydrogen bonds more strongly than glucose","Water is nonpolar, so it dissolves only small molecules"],1,"Solubility in water needs solute-water attractions that can replace water-water hydrogen bonds. Glucose is covered in O−H groups, so it hydrogen bonds extensively with water. Cholesterol is mostly C−H: its single O−H cannot drag the big nonpolar part into water.",["Like dissolves like.","Count O−H groups relative to the size of the molecule.","Cholesterol is mostly hydrocarbon."]],
["m94-a-001",94,1,3,"Trap","mcq","Report the result of (12.50 − 3.1) × 2.000 with the correct number of significant figures.",["18.8","18.80","19","18.800","20."],2,"Subtraction first: 12.50 − 3.1 = 9.4, kept to ONE decimal place (3.1 has one), so it has 2 sig figs. Then 9.4 × 2.000 = 18.8, which must be reported to 2 sig figs: 19. Carrying 18.8 or 18.80 ignores the subtraction rule; 20. rounds to too few.",["Mixed operations: do the subtraction and note its sig figs.","Subtraction keeps the fewest DECIMAL PLACES.","9.4 has two sig figs."]],
["m94-a-002",94,1,2,"Calculation","mcq","Ethanol has a density of 0.791 g/mL. What is its density in kg/m³?",["791 kg/m³","0.791 kg/m³","7.91 × 10⁻⁴ kg/m³","7.91 × 10⁵ kg/m³","79.1 kg/m³"],0,"0.791 g/mL × (1 kg/1000 g) × (10⁶ mL/1 m³) = 791 kg/m³. 1 m³ = (100 cm)³ = 10⁶ cm³ = 10⁶ mL - cubing the factor is the step people miss, and it is what separates 791 from 0.791 or 7.91 × 10⁻⁴.",["Two conversions: g → kg and mL → m³.","1 m³ = 10⁶ mL (cube the 100 cm/m).","0.791 × 10⁶ ÷ 1000."]],
["m94-a-003",94,2,2,"Concept","mcq","How many protons, neutrons and electrons are in the ion ⁵⁶Fe³⁺?",["26 p, 30 n, 29 e⁻","26 p, 56 n, 23 e⁻","23 p, 30 n, 26 e⁻","26 p, 30 n, 23 e⁻","30 p, 26 n, 23 e⁻"],3,"Fe has Z = 26 protons. Neutrons = 56 − 26 = 30. A 3+ ion has LOST three electrons: 26 − 3 = 23. 29 electrons adds the charge instead of subtracting it; 56 neutrons confuses A with N.",["Z comes from the periodic table.","N = A − Z; electrons = Z − charge.","26 − 3 electrons."]],
["m94-a-004",94,2,1,"Concept","mcq","What is the name of Fe₂(SO₄)₃?",["iron(II) sulfate","iron(III) sulfate","diiron trisulfate","iron(III) sulfite","iron sulfide"],1,"Three SO₄²⁻ carry 6− in total, shared between two Fe, so each Fe is 3+: iron(III). SO₄²⁻ is sulfate (SO₃²⁻ is sulfite, S²⁻ sulfide). Ionic compounds do not use Greek prefixes.",["Find the charge on each Fe from the anion charge.","3 × (2−) = 6− over 2 Fe atoms.","Fe³⁺ with SO₄²⁻."]],
["m94-a-005",94,2,3,"Calculation","mcq","Gallium (average atomic mass 69.723 amu) has two isotopes: ⁶⁹Ga (68.926 amu) and ⁷¹Ga (70.925 amu). What is the percent abundance of ⁶⁹Ga?",["39.9%","50.0%","60.1%","69.7%","98.9%"],2,"Let x = fraction of ⁶⁹Ga: 68.926x + 70.925(1 − x) = 69.723 → 70.925 − 1.999x = 69.723 → x = 1.202/1.999 = 0.6013 = 60.1%. Check: the average (69.723) is closer to 68.926, so ⁶⁹Ga must be the more abundant isotope. 39.9% is ⁷¹Ga.",["Average mass = Σ(mass × fraction).","Let the two fractions be x and 1 − x.","Solve 68.926x + 70.925(1 − x) = 69.723."]],
["m94-a-006",94,3,3,"Calculation","mcq","A compound is 40.0% C, 6.71% H and 53.3% O by mass, and its molar mass is 180.16 g/mol. What is its molecular formula?",["CH₂O","C₆H₁₂O₆","C₂H₄O₂","C₃H₆O₃","C₆H₆O₆"],1,"Per 100 g: C 40.0/12.01 = 3.33 mol; H 6.71/1.008 = 6.66 mol; O 53.3/16.00 = 3.33 mol → ratio 1 : 2 : 1, empirical formula CH₂O (30.03 g/mol). 180.16/30.03 = 6.00, so the molecular formula is C₆H₁₂O₆. CH₂O is only the empirical formula.",["Assume 100 g and convert each mass to moles.","Divide by the smallest to get the empirical formula.","Molar mass ÷ empirical-formula mass = 6."]],
["m94-a-007",94,4,3,"Exam","mcq","For 2Al(s) + 3Cl₂(g) → 2AlCl₃(s), 10.0 g of Al reacts with 35.0 g of Cl₂. What mass of AlCl₃ can form?",["98.7 g","65.8 g","49.4 g","45.0 g","43.9 g"],4,"From Al: 10.0/26.98 = 0.3706 mol Al → 0.3706 mol AlCl₃ (2 : 2). From Cl₂: 35.0/70.90 = 0.4937 mol Cl₂ × 2/3 = 0.3291 mol AlCl₃. Cl₂ makes less, so it is limiting: 0.3291 mol × 133.33 g/mol = 43.9 g. 49.4 g treats Al as limiting; 45.0 g just adds the masses (only true if nothing were left over).",["Find moles of product from EACH reactant.","The reactant that makes less product is limiting.","Cl₂: 0.4937 mol × 2/3."]],
["m94-a-008",94,5,2,"Calculation","mcq","25.0 mL of 6.00 M HCl is diluted with water to a final volume of 500.0 mL. What is the new concentration?",["0.300 M","120. M","0.150 M","3.00 M","0.0300 M"],0,"M₁V₁ = M₂V₂: M₂ = (6.00 M)(25.0 mL)/(500.0 mL) = 0.300 M. 120 M inverts the volumes (a dilution cannot raise the concentration).",["Moles of HCl do not change on dilution.","M₁V₁ = M₂V₂.","6.00 × 25.0 ÷ 500.0."]],
["m94-a-009",94,6,3,"Calculation","num","A 0.4512 g sample of KHP (KHC₈H₄O₄, 204.22 g/mol, one acidic H) is titrated to the equivalence point with 22.35 mL of NaOH. What is the molarity of the NaOH? (Enter a number in M.)",["0.09885 M"],0.09885378250458941,"n(KHP) = 0.4512/204.22 = 2.209 × 10⁻³ mol = n(NaOH) (1 : 1). M = 2.209 × 10⁻³ mol ÷ 0.02235 L = 0.09885 M.",["At the equivalence point mol OH⁻ = mol acidic H.","KHP : NaOH = 1 : 1; convert mL to L.","0.002209 mol ÷ 0.02235 L."]],
["m94-a-010",94,6,2,"Exam","mcq","What is the net ionic equation when aqueous Pb(NO₃)₂ and KI are mixed?",["K⁺(aq) + NO₃⁻(aq) → KNO₃(s)","Pb(NO₃)₂(aq) + 2KI(aq) → PbI₂(s) + 2KNO₃(aq)","Pb²⁺(aq) + 2I⁻(aq) → PbI₂(s)","Pb²⁺(aq) + I⁻(aq) → PbI(s)","No reaction occurs"],2,"Iodides are soluble EXCEPT with Ag⁺, Pb²⁺, Cu⁺ and Hg₂²⁺, so PbI₂ precipitates; KNO₃ is soluble (K⁺ and NO₃⁻ are spectators). The net ionic equation keeps only the species that change. The full Pb(NO₃)₂ + 2KI equation is the molecular equation, not the net ionic one; PbI has the wrong formula; KNO₃ is soluble.",["Which product is insoluble?","Pb²⁺ is an exception for halides.","Cross out K⁺ and NO₃⁻."]],
["m94-a-011",94,26,4,"Exam","mcq","A 0.500 g piece of Mg is dropped into 50.0 mL of 0.300 M HCl: Mg(s) + 2HCl(aq) → MgCl₂(aq) + H₂(g). What volume of H₂ forms at 25 °C and 1.00 atm?",["0.0154 L","0.168 L","0.367 L","0.183 L","0.503 L"],3,"n(Mg) = 0.500/24.31 = 0.02057 mol; n(HCl) = 0.0500 L × 0.300 M = 0.0150 mol. Mg could make 0.02057 mol H₂ but HCl only 0.0150/2 = 0.00750 mol - HCl is limiting. V = nRT/P = (0.00750)(0.08206)(298.15)/1.00 = 0.183 L. 0.503 L assumes Mg is limiting; 0.367 L forgets the 2 : 1 ratio; 22.4 L/mol applies only at STP.",["Two reactant amounts: find the limiting one.","mol HCl = M × V; H₂ = HCl ÷ 2.","HCl gives 0.00750 mol H₂."]],
["m94-a-012",94,26,4,"Exam","mcq","For N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) → 2NH₃(g), 5.00 L of N₂ and 12.0 L of H₂, both at 25 °C and 1.00 atm, react as completely as possible. What mass of NH₃ forms?",["2.78 g","5.57 g","6.08 g","6.96 g","8.35 g"],1,"5.00 L N₂ would need 15.0 L H₂ (1 : 3 by volume), but only 12.0 L is present, so H₂ is limiting. n(H₂) = PV/RT = 12.0/(0.08206 × 298.15) = 0.4905 mol → NH₃ = 0.4905 × 2/3 = 0.3270 mol × 17.03 g/mol = 5.57 g. Treating N₂ as limiting, using 22.4 L/mol at 25 °C, or skipping the 2/3 ratio gives the others.",["Find the limiting gas using the 1 : 3 volume ratio.","Then PV = nRT for the limiting gas, and the mole ratio to NH₃.","H₂ is limiting: 0.4905 mol."]],
["m94-a-013",94,9,4,"Exam","mcq","How much heat is released when 5.00 L of CH₄ at STP burns completely: CH₄(g) + 2O₂(g) → CO₂(g) + 2H₂O(l)? (ΔH°f, kJ/mol: CH₄(g) −74.9, CO₂(g) −393.5, H₂O(l) −285.8)",["135 kJ","179 kJ","890. kJ","4.45 × 10³ kJ","199 kJ"],4,"ΔH°rxn = [−393.5 + 2(−285.8)] − [−74.9] = −890.2 kJ per mol CH₄. n(CH₄) = 5.00 L ÷ 22.4 L/mol = 0.2232 mol. Heat released = 0.2232 × 890.2 = 199 kJ. Using H₂O(g) (−241.8), forgetting the 2 on water, stopping at the per-mole value, or treating 5.00 L as 5.00 mol gives the others.",["Two parts: ΔH per mole from ΔH°f, and moles of CH₄ from the gas volume.","Read the phase of water; multiply by its coefficient.","ΔH = −890.2 kJ/mol; n = 0.2232 mol."]],
["m94-a-014",94,8,4,"Exam","num","Burning propane releases 2219.9 kJ per mole (C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O(l)). What mass of water can be warmed by 50.0 °C by burning 2.00 L of propane measured at 25 °C and 1.00 atm, if all the heat goes into the water? (c(water) = 4.184 J/(g·°C); enter a number in kg.)",["0.867 kg"],0.8674319219098776,"n(C₃H₈) = PV/RT = 2.00/(0.08206 × 298.15) = 0.08175 mol. Heat = 0.08175 × 2219.9 kJ = 181.5 kJ = 1.815 × 10⁵ J. m = q/(cΔT) = 1.815 × 10⁵/(4.184 × 50.0) = 867.4 g = 0.867 kg.",["Gas volume → moles → heat → mass of water.","q(water) = m c ΔT with q in joules.","Heat ≈ 181.5 kJ."]],
["m94-a-015",94,14,4,"Exam","mcq","The first ionization energy of sodium is 496 kJ/mol. What is the LONGEST wavelength of light whose photons can ionize a single gaseous Na atom?",["2.41 × 10⁵ nm","4.00 × 10⁻²² nm","121 nm","Any wavelength works if the light is bright enough","241 nm"],4,"One photon ionizes one atom, so use the energy PER ATOM: 496 × 10³ J/mol ÷ 6.022 × 10²³ = 8.236 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. λ = hc/E = (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴)(2.998 × 10⁸)/8.236 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 2.412 × 10⁻⁷ m = 241 nm (ultraviolet). Leaving kJ unconverted or skipping Avogadro's number gives the absurd values. A 121 nm photon could also ionize Na, but it is not the LONGEST wavelength that works. Brightness never substitutes for photon energy (the photoelectric threshold).",["A photon acts on ONE atom: convert kJ/mol to J/atom.","× 1000, ÷ 6.022 × 10²³, then λ = hc/E.","E ≈ 8.24 × 10⁻¹⁹ J per atom."]],
["m94-a-016",94,11,3,"Calculation","mcq","Using the Bohr model (Eₙ = −2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J/n²), how much energy is needed to ionize one MOLE of hydrogen atoms that are all in the n = 2 state?",["1.31 × 10³ kJ/mol","985 kJ/mol","656 kJ/mol","328 kJ/mol","5.45 × 10⁻²² kJ/mol"],3,"Ionization takes the electron from n = 2 to n = ∞: ΔE = 0 − (−2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸/2²) = 5.45 × 10⁻¹⁹ J per atom. Per mole: 5.45 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 6.022 × 10²³ = 3.282 × 10⁵ J = 328 kJ/mol. 1.31 × 10³ kJ/mol starts from n = 1; 985 kJ/mol is the 1 → 2 excitation; dividing by n instead of n² gives 656.",["Ionization = moving the electron to n = ∞.","E needed per atom = 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸/2².","Then × N_A and J → kJ."]],
["m94-a-017",94,11,3,"Calculation","mcq","What is the wavelength of the photon emitted when a hydrogen electron falls from n = 3 to n = 2? (Eₙ = −2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J/n²)",["103 nm","364 nm","547 nm","656 nm","1.87 × 10³ nm"],3,"|ΔE| = 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ (1/2² − 1/3²) = 3.028 × 10⁻¹⁹ J per photon. λ = hc/|ΔE| = 6.561 × 10⁻⁷ m = 656 nm, the red Balmer line. Using 1/n instead of 1/n² gives 547 nm; the 3 → 1 and 4 → 3 transitions give the UV and IR choices.",["ΔE = 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ (1/n²final − 1/n²initial).","Square the n values.","λ = hc/|ΔE|; convert m to nm."]],
["m94-a-018",94,14,3,"Exam","mcq","Rank S²⁻, Cl⁻, K⁺ and Ca²⁺ in order of INCREASING radius.",["Ca²⁺ < K⁺ < Cl⁻ < S²⁻","S²⁻ < Cl⁻ < K⁺ < Ca²⁺","K⁺ < Ca²⁺ < S²⁻ < Cl⁻","Cl⁻ < S²⁻ < Ca²⁺ < K⁺","They are all the same size because they are isoelectronic"],0,"All four have 18 electrons ([Ar] configuration). With the same electrons, the ion with the most protons pulls them in hardest: Ca²⁺ (20 p) < K⁺ (19 p) < Cl⁻ (17 p) < S²⁻ (16 p). Isoelectronic does not mean equal size.",["Count the electrons in each ion.","Same electron count: compare nuclear charge.","More protons → smaller ion."]],
["m94-a-019",94,14,3,"Trap","mcq","What is the ground-state electron configuration of Mn²⁺, and how many unpaired electrons does it have?",["[Ar] 4s² 3d³, 3 unpaired","[Ar] 4s² 3d⁵, 5 unpaired","[Ar] 3d⁵, 1 unpaired","[Ar] 4s¹ 3d⁴, 5 unpaired","[Ar] 3d⁵, 5 unpaired"],4,"Mn is [Ar] 4s² 3d⁵. A transition-metal cation loses its 4s electrons FIRST: Mn²⁺ = [Ar] 3d⁵. By Hund's rule the five d electrons occupy five orbitals singly: 5 unpaired, paramagnetic. Removing 3d electrons first ([Ar] 4s² 3d³) is the classic trap; [Ar] 4s² 3d⁵ is neutral Mn.",["Start from neutral Mn.","Remove 4s electrons before 3d.","Five d electrons in five orbitals."]],
["m94-a-020",94,14,3,"Exam","mcq","An element X has successive ionization energies (kJ/mol) of 578, 1817, 2745 and 11,577. What is the formula of its oxide?",["XO","X₂O","X₂O₃","XO₂","X₃O₂"],2,"The huge jump comes after the THIRD electron, so X has 3 valence electrons and forms X³⁺ (this is Al). With O²⁻, charges balance as X₂O₃ (2 × 3+ = 3 × 2−).",["Find the big jump in ionization energy.","The jump marks the start of core electrons.","3 valence electrons → X³⁺."]],
["m94-a-021",94,13,2,"Concept","mcq","Which set of quantum numbers (n, l, mₗ, mₛ) could describe the highest-energy electron in a ground-state gallium atom?",["4, 0, 0, +½","3, 2, 0, +½","4, 1, 0, +½","4, 2, 1, +½","4, 1, 2, +½"],2,"Ga = [Ar] 4s² 3d¹⁰ 4p¹, so the last electron is in 4p: n = 4, l = 1, mₗ = −1, 0 or +1. (4, 1, 0, +½) fits. 4, 0 is a 4s electron; 3, 2 is 3d; 4, 2 is a 4d orbital (empty in Ga); mₗ = 2 is impossible when l = 1.",["Write gallium’s configuration.","p means l = 1.","mₗ must lie between −l and +l."]],
["m94-a-022",94,14,3,"Trap","mcq","Which element has the larger first ionization energy, nitrogen or oxygen, and why?",["Nitrogen, because oxygen’s fourth 2p electron is paired and the extra repulsion makes it easier to remove","Oxygen, because ionization energy always increases across a period","Oxygen, because it has more protons","Nitrogen, because nitrogen atoms are larger","They are equal because both are in period 2"],0,"N (1402 kJ/mol) beats O (1314 kJ/mol), an exception to the trend. N’s 2p³ has one electron in each p orbital; O’s 2p⁴ puts two electrons in one orbital, and their mutual repulsion makes one easier to remove. \"Always increases\" misses the dip; N is actually slightly larger, which would make it EASIER to ionize, so size cannot be the reason.",["This pair is one of the two dips in the IE trend.","Draw the 2p orbital diagrams for N and O.","Paired electrons repel."]],
["m94-a-023",94,18,4,"Exam","mcq","An element with the ground-state configuration [Ne] 3s² 3p⁴ forms a molecular tetrafluoride. What are the shape and polarity of that molecule?",["Tetrahedral, nonpolar","Square planar, nonpolar","Trigonal pyramidal, polar","Seesaw, polar","Tetrahedral, polar"],3,"[Ne] 3s² 3p⁴ is sulfur, so the molecule is SF₄. S has 6 valence electrons: 4 bonds + 1 lone pair = 5 electron groups (trigonal bipyramidal); the lone pair sits equatorial → seesaw. The asymmetric shape does not cancel the S−F dipoles → polar. \"Tetrahedral\" counts only the four bonds and forgets the lone pair.",["Identify the element from its configuration.","Count bonds plus lone pairs on the central atom.","Five groups with one lone pair."]],
["m94-a-024",94,18,3,"Exam","mcq","Which statement correctly describes sulfur trioxide (SO₃) and the sulfite ion (SO₃²⁻)?",["Both are trigonal planar","SO₃ is trigonal planar; SO₃²⁻ is trigonal pyramidal","Both are trigonal pyramidal","SO₃ is trigonal pyramidal; SO₃²⁻ is trigonal planar","SO₃ is T-shaped; SO₃²⁻ is trigonal planar"],1,"SO₃: 24 valence electrons; S has three bonding groups and no lone pair → trigonal planar (nonpolar). SO₃²⁻: 26 valence electrons; the extra two sit on S as a lone pair → 4 electron groups → trigonal pyramidal. Two extra electrons change the shape.",["Count valence electrons, adding 2 for the 2− charge.","Where do the extra electrons go?","A lone pair on S makes a pyramid."]],
["m94-a-025",94,19,4,"Exam","mcq","A gaseous oxide of sulfur has a density of 2.86 g/L at STP. What are the shape and polarity of its molecules?",["Trigonal planar and nonpolar (it is SO₃)","Linear and nonpolar (it is SO₂)","Bent and nonpolar (it is SO₂)","Trigonal pyramidal and polar (it is SO₃)","Bent and polar (it is SO₂)"],4,"M = d × 22.4 L/mol = 2.86 × 22.4 = 64.1 g/mol → SO₂ (64.06; SO₃ is 80.06). SO₂: S has two bonding groups + one lone pair (three electron groups) → bent, so the S−O dipoles do not cancel → polar. Drawing SO₂ like CO₂ (linear) forgets the lone pair on S.",["Molar mass from the density first.","At STP, M = d × 22.4.","Then the Lewis structure: count electron groups on S."]],
["m94-a-026",94,10,3,"Calculation","mcq","The Cl−Cl bond energy is 243 kJ/mol. What is the longest wavelength of light that can break a single Cl−Cl bond?",["8.17 × 10⁻²² nm","246 nm","492 nm","985 nm","4.92 × 10⁵ nm"],2,"Energy per bond = 243 × 10³ J/mol ÷ 6.022 × 10²³ = 4.035 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. λ = hc/E = (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴)(2.998 × 10⁸)/4.035 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 4.923 × 10⁻⁷ m = 492 nm (visible light - why chlorine reacts in sunlight). One photon breaks one bond, so the per-mole energy must become per-bond; doubling or halving for \"two atoms\" is not needed.",["One photon, one bond: you need J per bond.","× 1000 and ÷ N_A.","λ = hc/E, then m → nm."]],
["m94-a-027",94,26,2,"Exam","mcq","At 1 atm and 25 °C, which gas deviates MOST from ideal-gas behavior?",["CH₄","He","Ne","NH₃","N₂"],3,"KMT assumes no attractions. NH₃ molecules are polar and hydrogen bond, so their attractions are by far the strongest here (largest van der Waals a), making the measured pressure fall furthest below ideal. CH₄, N₂, Ne and He have only weak dispersion forces.",["Deviation comes from attractions (and size).","Which molecule has the strongest IMFs?","Look for hydrogen bonding."]],
["m94-a-028",94,26,3,"Trap","mcq","Sample A is helium at 300 K; sample B is SF₆ at 310 K. Which statement is correct?",["SF₆ molecules have the greater average kinetic energy, but helium atoms have the greater rms speed","Helium has both the greater average kinetic energy and the greater rms speed","SF₆ has both the greater average kinetic energy and the greater rms speed","The average kinetic energies are equal because both are ideal gases","Helium has the greater average kinetic energy, but SF₆ has the greater rms speed"],0,"Average KE depends only on T: 310 K beats 300 K, so SF₆ wins on kinetic energy. But u(rms) = √(3RT/M): helium's tiny molar mass (4.0 versus 146 g/mol) makes it about six times faster despite the slightly lower T.",["KE depends on T only; speed depends on T and M.","Compare T for KE.","Compare √(T/M) for speed."]],
["m94-a-029",94,26,1,"Concept","mcq","To calculate an rms speed in m/s with u(rms) = √(3RT/M), which R and which units of M must you use?",["R = 0.08206 L·atm/(mol·K) with M in g/mol","R = 8.314 J/(mol·K) with M in g/mol","R = 0.08206 L·atm/(mol·K) with M in kg/mol","Any value of R, as long as T is in °C","R = 8.314 J/(mol·K) with M in kg/mol"],4,"A joule is kg·m²/s², so J/(mol·K) × K ÷ (kg/mol) = m²/s², and the square root gives m/s. M in g/mol makes the answer √1000 too small; the L·atm value of R gives meaningless units.",["The answer must come out in m/s.","1 J = 1 kg·m²/s².","Match R in joules with M in kilograms."]],
["m94-a-030",94,25,3,"Trap","mcq","Calculate the moles of gas in a 2.0 L flask at 1.00 atm and 300. K, reported to the correct number of significant figures.",["0.0812 mol","0.08124 mol","0.081 mol","0.08 mol","0.0810 mol"],2,"n = PV/RT = (1.00)(2.0)/[(0.08206)(300.)] = 0.08124 mol. The least precise measurement is 2.0 L (2 sig figs), so n = 0.081 mol. Leading zeros do not count, so 0.081 has two sig figs and 0.08 only one.",["Find the measurement with the fewest sig figs.","Multiplication/division: fewest sig figs wins.","2.0 L has two."]],
["m94-a-031",94,25,3,"Calculation","mcq","A 0.0500 m³ tank holds gas at 202.6 kPa and 300. K. How many moles of gas does it hold? (R = 8.314 J/(mol·K) = 8.314 Pa·m³/(mol·K))",["4.06 × 10⁻³ mol","4.06 mol","0.0401 mol","411 mol","4.06 × 10³ mol"],1,"With R = 8.314 Pa·m³/(mol·K), P must be in Pa and V in m³: n = (202,600 Pa)(0.0500 m³)/[(8.314)(300.)] = 4.06 mol. Check with the other R: 202.6 kPa = 2.00 atm and 0.0500 m³ = 50.0 L, so n = (2.00)(50.0)/(0.08206 × 300.) = 4.06 mol - the same. Every wrong choice mismatches one unit with its R.",["Each R value comes with its own units.","Pa·m³ with 8.314, or atm·L with 0.08206.","202,600 × 0.0500 ÷ (8.314 × 300.)."]],
["m94-a-032",94,26,3,"Exam","mcq","A 2.45 L sample of HCl gas, measured at 25 °C and 1.00 atm, is dissolved in water to make 500.0 mL of solution. What is the molarity of the hydrochloric acid?",["0.200 M","2.00 × 10⁻⁴ M","0.0501 M","0.219 M","2.39 M"],0,"n(HCl) = PV/RT = (1.00)(2.45)/[(0.08206)(298.15)] = 0.1001 mol. M = 0.1001 mol ÷ 0.5000 L = 0.200 M. Using 22.4 L/mol (not STP), dividing by mL, or multiplying by the volume gives the others.",["Gas volume → moles with PV = nRT.","Molarity = mol ÷ litres of solution.","0.1001 mol ÷ 0.5000 L."]],
["m94-a-033",94,26,3,"Worksheet","mcq","Na₂CO₃(aq) + 2HCl(aq) → 2NaCl(aq) + H₂O(l) + CO₂(g). If 4.236 g of Na₂CO₃ reacts with excess HCl, what volume of CO₂ is released at 25 °C and 1.00 atm?",["0.0820 L","0.978 L","0.895 L","1.96 L","2.35 L"],1,"n(Na₂CO₃) = 4.236/105.99 = 0.03997 mol = n(CO₂) (1 : 1). V = nRT/P = (0.03997)(0.08206)(298.15)/1.00 = 0.978 L. 22.4 L/mol is STP only; the 2 belongs to HCl, not CO₂; 4.236 g is the mass of Na₂CO₃, not of CO₂.",["Moles of Na₂CO₃ first (M = 105.99 g/mol).","CO₂ : Na₂CO₃ = 1 : 1; then V = nRT/P.","n ≈ 0.0400 mol."]],
["m94-a-034",94,25,4,"Exam","mcq","A gaseous hydrocarbon is 82.6% C and 17.4% H by mass. A 1.20 g sample occupies 0.500 L at 25 °C and 1.01 atm. What is its molecular formula?",["C₂H₅","C₄H₁₀","C₃H₈","C₅H₁₂","C₄H₈"],1,"Empirical: C 82.6/12.01 = 6.88 mol, H 17.4/1.008 = 17.3 mol; ratio 1 : 2.5 = 2 : 5 → C₂H₅ (29.06 g/mol). Molar mass: n = PV/RT = (1.01)(0.500)/(0.08206 × 298.15) = 2.064 × 10⁻² mol, so M = 1.20/2.064 × 10⁻² = 58.1 g/mol. 58.1/29.06 = 2 → C₄H₁₀ (58.12 g/mol). C₂H₅ is only the empirical formula.",["Empirical formula from the percentages; molar mass from the gas data.","M = mRT/PV.","The molar mass is twice the empirical-formula mass."]],
["m94-a-035",94,26,3,"Calculation","mcq","An unknown gas effuses 0.672 times as fast as O₂ under the same conditions. Which gas is it?",["F₂","Br₂","SO₂","Ar","Cl₂"],4,"M(X) = M(O₂)/(rate ratio)² = 32.00/(0.672)² = 70.9 g/mol → Cl₂ (70.90). SO₂ is 64.06, Ar 39.95, F₂ 38.00, Br₂ 159.8.",["Slower than O₂ → heavier than O₂.","rate(X)/rate(O₂) = √(32.00/M(X)).","M = 32.00 ÷ 0.672²."]],
["m94-a-036",94,26,3,"Calculation","num","What volume of O₂ at STP is needed to burn 1.00 g of octane completely? 2C₈H₁₈(l) + 25O₂(g) → 16CO₂(g) + 18H₂O(l) (Enter a number in L.)",["2.45 L"],2.4513237148059948,"M(C₈H₁₈) = 8(12.01) + 18(1.008) = 114.22 g/mol, so n = 8.755 × 10⁻³ mol. O₂ = 8.755 × 10⁻³ × 25/2 = 0.1094 mol. At STP: 0.1094 × 22.4 = 2.45 L.",["grams octane → mol octane → mol O₂ → litres.","Ratio 25 O₂ : 2 C₈H₁₈.","22.4 L/mol at STP."]],
["m94-a-037",94,26,4,"Exam","num","A 0.270 g piece of aluminum reacts with excess HCl: 2Al(s) + 6HCl(aq) → 2AlCl₃(aq) + 3H₂(g). The H₂ is collected over water at 25 °C and a total pressure of 760. torr (vapor pressure of water at 25 °C = 23.8 torr). What volume of gas is collected? (Enter a number in mL.)",["379 mL"],379.13788525775675,"n(Al) = 0.270/26.98 = 1.001 × 10⁻² mol → H₂ = × 3/2 = 1.501 × 10⁻² mol. The H₂ supplies only part of the pressure: P(H₂) = 760. − 23.8 = 736.2 torr = 0.9687 atm. V = nRT/P = (1.501 × 10⁻²)(0.08206)(298.15)/0.9687 = 0.3791 L = 379 mL.",["Moles of H₂ from the mole ratio 3 H₂ : 2 Al.","Use the DRY H₂ pressure in PV = nRT.","P(H₂) = 736.2 torr."]],
["m94-a-038",94,26,4,"Exam","mcq","A rigid flask at constant temperature holds NO at 0.600 atm and O₂ at 0.500 atm. They react completely: 2NO(g) + O₂(g) → 2NO₂(g). What is the final total pressure?",["0.200 atm","0.600 atm","0.800 atm","1.10 atm","1.40 atm"],2,"Constant V and T: partial pressures behave like moles. NO is limiting: 0.600 atm NO uses 0.300 atm O₂ and makes 0.600 atm NO₂. O₂ left = 0.500 − 0.300 = 0.200 atm. Total = 0.600 + 0.200 = 0.800 atm. 1.10 atm ignores the reaction; 0.600 atm forgets the leftover O₂.",["Treat partial pressures like moles.","Find the limiting reactant from the 2 : 1 ratio.","Add the product pressure and the leftover reactant."]],
["m94-a-039",94,7,4,"Exam","mcq","A reaction in an open flask produces 0.0500 mol of H₂ gas at 25 °C against a constant 1.00 atm. How much work does the system do, with the correct sign? (1 L·atm = 101.3 J; ignore the volume of solids and liquids.)",["+124 J","−0.124 J","−1.22 J","−124 J","−1.24 × 10⁵ J"],3,"The gas produced pushes back the atmosphere: ΔV = nRT/P = (0.0500)(0.08206)(298.15)/1.00 = 1.223 L. w = −PΔV = −(1.00 atm)(1.223 L) = −1.223 L·atm × 101.3 J/(L·atm) = −124 J. Work done BY the system is negative; −1.22 J forgets to convert L·atm to J.",["w = −PΔV; the new gas is the volume change.","ΔV from PV = nRT.","Then L·atm → J with 101.3."]],
["m94-a-040",94,7,4,"Exam","mcq","For C₃H₈(g) + 5O₂(g) → 3CO₂(g) + 4H₂O(l) at 25 °C and constant pressure, what is the work w for the reaction of 1 mol of propane? (w = −PΔV = −Δn(gas)RT)",["−7.44 kJ","−2.48 kJ","+7.44 kJ","+12.4 kJ","0 kJ"],2,"Count only gases: Δn = 3 − (1 + 5) = −3 mol. w = −ΔnRT = −(−3)(8.314)(298.15) = +7436 J = +7.44 kJ. The gas volume shrinks, so the surroundings do work ON the system: w is positive. Counting H₂O(l) as a gas gives Δn = +1 (w = −2.48 kJ); counting only the 5 mol of O₂ consumed gives Δn = −5 (w = +12.4 kJ).",["Only gas moles change the volume.","Δn = moles gas products − moles gas reactants.","Δn = −3."]],
["m94-a-041",94,8,3,"Worksheet","num","A 50.0 g block of aluminum (c = 0.900 J/(g·°C)) at 95.0 °C is dropped into 100.0 g of water at 22.0 °C in an insulated cup. What is the final temperature? (Enter a number in °C.)",["29.1 °C"],29.088908070781184,"Heat lost by Al = heat gained by water: 50.0(0.900)(95.0 − T) = 100.0(4.184)(T − 22.0). 4275 − 45.0T = 418.4T − 9204.8, so 463.4T = 13,479.8 and T = 29.1 °C. It must land between 22.0 and 95.0 °C, close to the water's temperature because water's heat capacity is much larger.",["−q(metal) = q(water); both end at the same T.","Write m·c·ΔT for each with ΔT = final − initial.","Solve 45.0(95.0 − T) = 418.4(T − 22.0)."]],
["m94-a-042",94,9,3,"Calculation","mcq","N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) → 2NH₃(g). Using ΔH°f(NH₃, g) = −45.9 kJ/mol, how much heat is released when 10.0 L of N₂ at STP reacts with excess H₂?",["41.0 kJ","13.7 kJ","20.5 kJ","82.0 kJ","918 kJ"],0,"ΔH°rxn = 2(−45.9) − 0 = −91.8 kJ per mol N₂ (elements have ΔH°f = 0). n(N₂) = 10.0/22.4 = 0.4464 mol. Heat released = 0.4464 × 91.8 = 41.0 kJ. Forgetting the coefficient 2 on NH₃ halves it; treating 10.0 L as 10.0 mol, or as H₂, gives the others.",["ΔH°rxn from ΔH°f: products − reactants, with coefficients.","N₂ and H₂ are elements: ΔH°f = 0.","ΔH°rxn = −91.8 kJ per mol N₂."]],
["m94-a-043",94,28,4,"Exam","mcq","Polonium-210 (209.98 g/mol, t½ = 138.4 days) decays by α emission to stable ²⁰⁶Pb; each α particle becomes a He atom. A sealed sample starts with 1.00 g of ²¹⁰Po. What volume of He at STP has formed after 276.8 days?",["80.0 mL","26.7 mL","53.3 mL","107 mL","320. mL"],0,"276.8 days = 2 half-lives, so ¼ of the Po remains and ¾ (0.750 g) has decayed. Moles decayed = 0.750/209.98 = 3.572 × 10⁻³ mol, giving the same number of moles of He. V = 3.572 × 10⁻³ × 22.4 L/mol = 0.0800 L = 80.0 mL. Using the amount REMAINING, one half-life, or all of the sample gives the other volumes.",["How many half-lives have passed?","Moles of He = moles of Po that decayed.","¾ of 1.00 g decayed."]],
["m94-a-044",94,27,4,"Exam","mcq","When 1.00 mol of ²³⁸U decays completely to ²⁰⁶Pb, every α particle becomes a helium atom. What mass of helium forms? (He = 4.003 g/mol)",["4.00 g","32.0 g","24.0 g","56.0 g","128 g"],1,"The series ²³⁸U → ²⁰⁶Pb emits (238 − 206)/4 = 8 α particles (and 6 β⁻). So 1.00 mol U gives 8.00 mol He = 8.00 × 4.003 = 32.0 g. 24.0 g uses the number of β particles; 56.0 g counts all 14 particles as He; 128 g forgets to divide ΔA by 4.",["How many α particles in the ²³⁸U → ²⁰⁶Pb series?","ΔA ÷ 4 = number of α.","8 mol He × 4.003 g/mol."]],
["m94-a-045",94,27,3,"Calculation","mcq","Uranium-238 decays through a series to lead-206. Using mass numbers as molar masses, what mass of ²⁰⁶Pb forms when 1.00 g of ²³⁸U has decayed completely?",["1.00 g","1.16 g","0.134 g","0.433 g","0.866 g"],4,"1.00 g ÷ 238 g/mol = 4.20 × 10⁻³ mol U → the same number of moles of Pb (one Pb per U). Mass = 4.20 × 10⁻³ × 206 = 0.866 g. The missing 0.134 g left as α particles (and a tiny amount as energy). 1.16 g inverts the ratio; 0.433 g halves it as if one half-life had passed.",["One Pb atom forms per U atom.","Moles of U = moles of Pb.","1.00 × 206/238."]],
["m94-a-046",94,27,2,"Concept","mcq","Potassium-40 decays mainly by β⁻ emission. In which group of the periodic table is the daughter element?",["Group 1, the alkali metals","Group 2, the alkaline earth metals","Group 18, the noble gases","Group 17, the halogens","Group 3"],1,"⁴⁰₁₉K → ⁴⁰₂₀Ca + ⁰₋₁β. β⁻ raises Z by one, from potassium (Group 1) to calcium (Group 2). Argon (Group 18) is the product of ⁴⁰K's minor electron-capture branch, which lowers Z.",["β⁻ changes Z by +1.","Z = 20: calcium.","Calcium’s group."]],
["m94-a-047",94,27,3,"Exam","mcq","Iodine-131 undergoes β⁻ decay. What is the ground-state electron configuration of the neutral atom of the daughter element?",["[Kr] 5s² 4d¹⁰ 5p⁵","[Kr] 5s² 4d¹⁰ 5p⁴","[Xe] 6s¹","[Kr] 5s² 4d¹⁰ 5p⁶","[Kr] 5s² 4d¹⁰ 5p⁶ 6s²"],3,"¹³¹₅₃I → ¹³¹₅₄Xe + β⁻. The daughter is xenon (Z = 54), a noble gas: [Kr] 5s² 4d¹⁰ 5p⁶. 5p⁵ is iodine itself; 5p⁴ is tellurium (the direction a positron emitter would go); [Xe] 6s¹ is cesium.",["Identify the daughter first.","β⁻: Z + 1 = 54.","Xenon fills the 5p subshell."]],
["m94-a-048",94,28,4,"Exam","mcq","When a positron from ¹⁸F meets an electron in a PET scan, both annihilate into two γ photons of equal energy, each carrying the rest energy of one electron (m = 9.109 × 10⁻³¹ kg). What is the wavelength of each photon?",["4.12 × 10¹¹ m","2.43 × 10⁻³ m","7.27 × 10⁻⁴ m","2.43 × 10⁻¹² m","1.21 × 10⁻¹² m"],3,"E per photon = mc² = (9.109 × 10⁻³¹ kg)(2.998 × 10⁸ m/s)² = 8.187 × 10⁻¹⁴ J (= 0.511 MeV). λ = hc/E = (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴)(2.998 × 10⁸)/8.187 × 10⁻¹⁴ = 2.43 × 10⁻¹² m - a γ ray, shorter than an X-ray. Giving one photon both electrons' energy halves λ; forgetting to square c gives a nonsense value.",["Mass → energy (E = mc²), then energy → wavelength (E = hc/λ).","Each photon carries ONE electron’s rest energy.","E ≈ 8.19 × 10⁻¹⁴ J."]],
["m94-a-049",94,28,3,"Calculation","mcq","Cobalt-60 emits a γ photon of energy 1.33 MeV. What is its wavelength? (1 MeV = 1.602 × 10⁻¹³ J)",["1.49 × 10⁻²⁵ m","9.32 × 10⁻¹³ m","9.32 × 10⁻¹⁰ m","9.32 × 10⁻⁷ m","1.07 × 10¹² m"],1,"E = 1.33 MeV × 1.602 × 10⁻¹³ J/MeV = 2.131 × 10⁻¹³ J. λ = hc/E = (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴)(2.998 × 10⁸)/2.131 × 10⁻¹³ = 9.32 × 10⁻¹³ m. Treating MeV as eV or keV, or skipping the conversion to joules, gives the other choices.",["Convert MeV to J first.","λ = hc/E.","E ≈ 2.13 × 10⁻¹³ J."]],
["m94-a-050",94,28,4,"Exam","mcq","Fission of 1.00 g of ²³⁵U releases about 8.2 × 10⁷ kJ. What mass of methane (16.04 g/mol, 890 kJ released per mole burned) would have to burn to release the same energy?",["1.5 × 10⁶ kg","92 kg","1.5 × 10³ kg","5.7 kg","1.5 kg"],2,"Moles of CH₄ = 8.2 × 10⁷ kJ ÷ 890 kJ/mol = 9.2 × 10⁴ mol. Mass = 9.2 × 10⁴ × 16.04 g/mol = 1.5 × 10⁶ g = 1.5 × 10³ kg - about a tonne and a half of fuel to match one gram of uranium. 92 kg skips the molar mass; 5.7 kg divides by the molar mass; 1.5 × 10⁶ kg forgets that 1000 g = 1 kg.",["Energy ÷ (kJ per mol) = moles.","Moles × molar mass = grams.","Then convert g to kg (2 sig figs)."]],
["m94-a-051",94,25,4,"Exam","mcq","Which sample contains the greatest number of molecules?",["10.0 L of N₂ at STP","22.0 g of CO₂","10.0 g of H₂O","1.00 × 10²³ molecules of CO₂","5.00 L of H₂ at 25 °C and 2.00 atm"],2,"Convert all to moles. H₂O: 10.0/18.02 = 0.555 mol. N₂: 10.0/22.4 = 0.446 mol. CO₂: 22.0/44.01 = 0.500 mol. 1.00 × 10²³ ÷ 6.022 × 10²³ = 0.166 mol. H₂: PV/RT = (2.00)(5.00)/(0.08206 × 298.15) = 0.409 mol. Water wins. Grams, litres and counts must all go through moles.",["Put every sample in moles.","Gases: PV = nRT (or 22.4 L/mol only at STP).","Water: 10.0 ÷ 18.02."]],
["m94-a-052",94,22,3,"Calculation","mcq","Polonium (209.0 g/mol), one of the elements Marie Curie discovered, is the only element that crystallizes in a simple cubic lattice. Its edge length is 336 pm. What is its density?",["9.15 g/cm³","18.3 g/cm³","36.6 g/cm³","73.2 g/cm³","9.15 × 10⁶ g/cm³"],0,"Simple cubic: 8 corners × ⅛ = 1 atom per cell. Mass = 209.0/6.022 × 10²³ = 3.471 × 10⁻²² g. a = 336 pm = 3.36 × 10⁻⁸ cm, V = a³ = 3.793 × 10⁻²³ cm³. d = 9.15 g/cm³. 2 and 4 atoms are BCC and FCC; 8 counts every corner as a whole atom.",["How many atoms belong to a simple cubic cell?","d = (atoms × M/N_A) ÷ a³, a in cm.","1 pm = 10⁻¹⁰ cm."]]
]);
